00:08:31.917 --> 00:08:33.732
"I've got a job that only you can do."

00:08:33.732 --> 00:08:35.720
- Did you apply for this?

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.438
- No, I was just his intern.

00:08:37.438 --> 00:08:39.200
I was, like, getting
him lunches and coffee.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:41.906
And I was just doing some, like,

00:08:41.906 --> 00:08:43.646
research for metadata, kind of thing.

00:08:43.646 --> 00:08:44.556
- Was it a thing where,

00:08:44.556 --> 00:08:46.607
"I don't want this to
seem too misogynistic.

00:08:46.607 --> 00:08:48.348
"Could you, like, spice
it up a little bit?"

00:08:48.348 --> 00:08:50.238
- That was actually, exactly it.

00:08:50.238 --> 00:08:51.277
- Okay, okay.

00:08:51.277 --> 00:08:54.402
- Like, I can't even tell
you how exact that was.

00:08:54.402 --> 00:08:55.367
- I know you, Phil.

00:08:55.367 --> 00:08:56.429
- Yo, yo, yo, Ashley,

00:08:56.429 --> 00:08:58.136
do you want to host a new segment for us?

00:08:58.136 --> 00:08:59.886
(all laughing)

00:08:59.886 --> 00:09:02.936
- And it literally, like,
the segment was called,

00:09:02.936 --> 00:09:06.376
"Intern Reina Brings In The Sexy Ladies."

00:09:06.376 --> 00:09:08.108
(guys laughing)

00:09:08.108 --> 00:09:10.419
- You can't make this shit up.

00:09:10.419 --> 00:09:11.967
- So, that happened.

00:09:11.967 --> 00:09:14.068
- That's probably your past
that Phil kind of wants to,

00:09:14.068 --> 00:09:15.807
like, brush under the rug, right?

00:09:15.807 --> 00:09:18.376
- That is the most 2012
sounding title I've ever heard.

00:09:18.376 --> 00:09:19.226
- It is!

00:09:19.226 --> 00:09:22.476
- So, that's when Phil started
jumping right into it, huh?

00:09:22.476 --> 00:09:24.056
- I had to say, "Sup, Nation?"

00:09:24.056 --> 00:09:27.215
That's why it like rings in my head.

00:09:27.215 --> 00:09:29.455
But it was actually a lot of fun.

00:09:29.455 --> 00:09:33.044
And Darren was the cameraman at the time,

00:09:33.044 --> 00:09:33.877
he was the producer.

00:09:33.877 --> 00:09:35.460
And he was so good.

00:09:36.294 --> 00:09:37.787
And he was, like, so professional.

00:09:37.787 --> 00:09:39.717
And he really, I don't know,

00:09:39.717 --> 00:09:41.668
he, like, brought me
into this new world of

00:09:41.668 --> 00:09:44.747
being not an intern but talking on camera.

00:09:44.747 --> 00:09:48.126
And I was so nervous, but it
was just, like, so much fun.

00:09:48.126 --> 00:09:49.586
And also, it was talking
about sexy ladies!

00:09:49.586 --> 00:09:52.847
Which I was like, "Yeah! I'll do that!"

00:09:52.847 --> 00:09:54.079
- You could be talking about worse things.

00:09:54.079 --> 00:09:54.912
- Yeah!

00:09:54.912 --> 00:09:56.577
The worst part about it though,

00:09:56.577 --> 00:09:59.367
was that it was this new fresh 18 girl,

00:09:59.367 --> 00:10:04.069
who was being, I guess, like,
being Googled before that,

00:10:04.069 --> 00:10:05.877
but she like just turned 18, so it's like,

00:10:05.877 --> 00:10:07.195
"It's legal now!"

00:10:07.195 --> 00:10:09.187
And I had to like do that.

00:10:09.187 --> 00:10:10.020
Yeah, so.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:10.869
- Oof.

00:10:10.869 --> 00:10:12.599
- YouTube 10 years ago was
a different planet, man.

00:10:12.599 --> 00:10:13.432
- It was.

00:10:13.432 --> 00:10:16.049
- And that was like monetized.

00:10:16.049 --> 00:10:17.108
- Can only imagine.

00:10:17.108 --> 00:10:19.527
It makes me feel sad that
when you refer to SourceFed,

00:10:19.527 --> 00:10:21.418
you're like, "Oh, it's
an OG YouTube channel."

00:10:21.418 --> 00:10:24.110
And I'm just like, "Oh shit,
I guess it is, isn't it?

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:25.378
"Oh, fuck!"

00:10:25.378 --> 00:10:26.960
- You're an OG YouTube channel.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.179
- I keep forgetting!

00:10:28.179 --> 00:10:30.186
'Cause like, I've been
following Phil since, like,

00:10:30.186 --> 00:10:32.116
close to when he started, right?

00:10:32.116 --> 00:10:33.999
So, I remember when he
announced SourceFed,

00:10:33.999 --> 00:10:36.739
I was like, "Ooh, what's this
new venture that he's doing?

00:10:36.739 --> 00:10:37.639
"This sounds exciting."

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:39.830
And now that it's like
now an OG dead channel

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.018
that is just buried in people's memories,

00:10:42.018 --> 00:10:43.879
like, that makes me feel a bit sad!

00:10:43.879 --> 00:10:45.870
I'm just having this
realization live on camera.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.107
- I used to watch SourceFed a lot.

00:10:48.107 --> 00:10:49.249
- Oh my god.

00:10:49.249 --> 00:10:50.859
- Like, fuckin' like, yeah.

00:10:50.859 --> 00:10:52.488
I used to watch it a lot.

00:10:52.488 --> 00:10:54.254
That was like, at one point, I would say

00:10:54.254 --> 00:10:55.607
it was probably one of my
favorite channels to watch.

00:10:55.607 --> 00:10:56.501
- [Reina] Wow!

00:10:56.501 --> 00:10:58.634
- Just because it's,
again, it's, you know,

00:10:58.634 --> 00:10:59.467
it wasn't like as, you know,

00:10:59.467 --> 00:11:01.192
I watch Philly D. as well, obviously,

00:11:01.192 --> 00:11:02.191
for, like, the news stuff.

00:11:02.191 --> 00:11:04.680
But it was also just like,
it was edutainment, right?

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.513
- Yes! Exactly.

00:11:05.513 --> 00:11:07.124
- That was like the big
selling point for it.

00:11:07.124 --> 00:11:08.859
It was like, you get to learn some shit

00:11:08.859 --> 00:11:10.818
but at the same time, you can
have fun with these comedians.

00:11:10.818 --> 00:11:11.651
(all agreeing)

00:11:11.651 --> 00:11:13.556
- You kind of remember
it because it's funny.

00:11:13.556 --> 00:11:15.320
I think that was also
the other point of it.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.418
Like, it's shareable
because it's not dark,

00:11:17.418 --> 00:11:18.817
and it's also funny,

00:11:18.817 --> 00:11:20.627
so it makes sense to, like,

00:11:20.627 --> 00:11:22.441
put all over Facebook, kind of thing.

00:11:22.441 --> 00:11:24.075
Everybody else can watch it
and be entertained by it.

00:11:24.075 --> 00:11:26.151
So, yeah, it was like a brilliant idea

00:11:26.151 --> 00:11:28.619
and they started making
other sub channels,

00:11:28.619 --> 00:11:31.593
like SourceFedNERD and then
I ran their gaming channel,

00:11:31.593 --> 00:11:33.651
Super Panic Frenzy, with Steven Suptic.

00:11:33.651 --> 00:11:36.099
And that was like my golden time, I'd say.

00:11:36.099 --> 00:11:37.387
That was when I was already, finally,

00:11:37.387 --> 00:11:39.686
comfortable being on
camera, it was really fun.

00:11:39.686 --> 00:11:42.284
And then I left for Crunchyroll.

00:11:42.284 --> 00:11:43.976
So, (laughs)

00:11:43.976 --> 00:11:46.593
- So, what made you leave?

00:11:46.593 --> 00:11:50.024
- I was really heartbroken
that they canceled

00:11:50.024 --> 00:11:52.837
Super Panic Frenzy, my gaming channel,

00:11:52.837 --> 00:11:54.854
because we were hitting all the numbers.

00:11:54.854 --> 00:11:55.933
Like, hard.

00:11:55.933 --> 00:11:58.087
We were completely setting all the goals.

00:11:58.087 --> 00:11:59.598
And at the time,

00:11:59.598 --> 00:12:02.870
we were owned by Discovery
Digital Networks.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:03.703
- Yeah.

00:12:03.703 --> 00:12:05.470
- I remember that whole thing.

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:06.524
- I remember SourceFed ending

00:12:06.524 --> 00:12:07.755
and then they tried to continue it

00:12:07.755 --> 00:12:09.857
under a different name
or something, right?

00:12:09.857 --> 00:12:10.690
(guys agreeing)

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:12.292
- That's, yeah, that was bad.

00:12:12.292 --> 00:12:14.875
I think it's, like, a completely
different company now.

00:12:14.875 --> 00:12:17.138
But right when SourceFed, like,

00:12:17.138 --> 00:12:18.879
when Discovery let go of SourceFed,

00:12:18.879 --> 00:12:20.606
somebody tried to pick
it up and kind of, like,

00:12:20.606 --> 00:12:23.108
almost zombie reanimate it, like,

00:12:23.108 --> 00:12:24.887
still using the audience and usurping it.

00:12:24.887 --> 00:12:26.629
- Yeah, like, they took the same channel,

00:12:26.629 --> 00:12:29.928
re-named it and hired different hosts

00:12:29.928 --> 00:12:31.859
to continue on the show.

00:12:31.859 --> 00:12:32.956
- As though, yeah.

00:12:32.956 --> 00:12:35.546
It felt very, like, puppeted and strange.

00:12:35.546 --> 00:12:37.438
- It kind of felt like you
went to a friend's house

00:12:37.438 --> 00:12:39.613
and there was just a
stranger sitting there

00:12:39.613 --> 00:12:41.415
pretending to be your friend.
- This is my house now.

00:12:41.415 --> 00:12:43.412
- Wait a minute, what's going on?

00:12:43.412 --> 00:12:44.515
I'm at Connor's house.

00:12:44.515 --> 00:12:45.592
- Welcome back, Garnt!

00:12:45.592 --> 00:12:47.236
(all laughing)

00:12:47.236 --> 00:12:48.515
Good to see you back here.

00:12:48.515 --> 00:12:49.893
- It was like, "Hold
on, that's not Connor,

00:12:49.893 --> 00:12:51.214
"that's Connor animatronic!"

00:12:51.214 --> 00:12:53.281
(all laughing)

00:12:53.281 --> 00:12:54.613
- That's so worrying, oh my god.

00:12:54.613 --> 00:12:56.755
I didn't follow any of the
SourceFed stuff, at all.

00:12:56.755 --> 00:12:57.588
I think, on YouTube,

00:12:57.588 --> 00:12:59.114
I was not watching that
kind of stuff then.

00:12:59.114 --> 00:13:00.923
I think 'cause I was still
watching game-play stuff.

00:13:00.923 --> 00:13:03.522
I was exclusively in Call
of Duty walk throughs

00:13:03.522 --> 00:13:04.782
or some stupid shit.

00:13:04.782 --> 00:13:08.023
- That's not stupid shit,
I was into that too.

00:13:08.023 --> 00:13:08.904
- Don't lie.

00:13:08.904 --> 00:13:09.737
(Reina laughing)

00:13:09.737 --> 00:13:10.851
- Can we talk about SourceFed Anime?

00:13:10.851 --> 00:13:14.163
Or what was the anime
show you had on SourceFed?

00:13:14.163 --> 00:13:15.251
- Oh, SourceFed Animated.

00:13:15.251 --> 00:13:16.775
- Yeah.
- Yeah! That was fantastic!

00:13:16.775 --> 00:13:20.108
We had Andre, this independent animator,

00:13:21.238 --> 00:13:24.634
make animations of short
clips from things like

00:13:24.634 --> 00:13:26.474
our podcasts and our table talks.

00:13:26.474 --> 00:13:29.075
And they were very good
because he was very good.

00:13:29.075 --> 00:13:30.415
The animator did everything,

00:13:30.415 --> 00:13:32.424
he like really expanded
the comedy on our behalf.

00:13:32.424 --> 00:13:34.840
- Oh, not SourceFed Animated.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:35.904
- Anime Club?

00:13:35.904 --> 00:13:37.421
- Oh, Anime Club!

00:13:37.421 --> 00:13:40.034
Oh, okay, sorry! I should've
like figured that out.

00:13:40.034 --> 00:13:40.867
- I know none of these.

00:13:40.867 --> 00:13:42.619
- I should've figured that out, yeah.

00:13:42.619 --> 00:13:44.579
- With, what was it? William Hanes, is it?

00:13:44.579 --> 00:13:46.245
- William Hanes.

00:13:46.245 --> 00:13:48.384
- I remember when that came out,

00:13:48.384 --> 00:13:52.712
and I was like, "Shit, I'm
going to be out of business."

00:13:52.712 --> 00:13:56.375
- Yeah, actually, so, Anime
Club existed before me

00:13:56.375 --> 00:13:58.673
and Meg Turney was doing it.

00:13:58.673 --> 00:13:59.506
(guys agreeing)

00:13:59.506 --> 00:14:00.633
and she kind of did it like--

00:14:00.633 --> 00:14:03.772
- All these names sound so
familiar, but not quite.

00:14:03.772 --> 00:14:04.829
I don't like have a face to put to them.

00:14:04.829 --> 00:14:07.841
- Well, Meg Turney now
is like a cosplayer.

00:14:07.841 --> 00:14:10.021
- Yeah and she's a sexy model.

00:14:10.021 --> 00:14:11.751
And yeah, she's doing great.

00:14:11.751 --> 00:14:13.772
And we still keep in
touch, I miss her a lot.

00:14:13.772 --> 00:14:14.983
She misses Japan.

00:14:14.983 --> 00:14:16.660
But yeah, so she started it

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:20.270
and I do believe they wanted to expand it,

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:24.461
and it would obviously make
sense if somebody who, like,

00:14:24.461 --> 00:14:27.310
has some kind of professional
experience with anime

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.123
was on it as well, on top
of like being Japanese,

00:14:29.123 --> 00:14:30.779
that's kind of a no-brainer there.

00:14:30.779 --> 00:14:34.620
So, I was legitimately
hired mainly to do that,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:35.969
to fill that role.

00:14:35.969 --> 00:14:39.331
And it was just when
she was on her way out.

00:14:39.331 --> 00:14:41.351
So, I basically replaced her.

00:14:41.351 --> 00:14:44.283
And yeah, and then Will
and I took it over.

00:14:44.283 --> 00:14:48.291
But, it was fun, but it
was really difficult.

00:14:48.291 --> 00:14:50.262
'Cause the style was supposed to be

00:14:50.262 --> 00:14:54.171
that I was a so-called expert,
kind of teaching the novice.

00:14:54.171 --> 00:14:55.951
And he has all these questions

00:14:55.951 --> 00:14:58.578
but they're so, very
left-field that, like,

00:14:58.578 --> 00:15:01.483
I didn't know how to kind
of bring it back. (laughs)

00:15:01.483 --> 00:15:05.003
- Because I think that,
all respect to Meg Turney,

00:15:05.003 --> 00:15:08.081
I remember when she was
handling Anime Club,

00:15:08.081 --> 00:15:11.414
which was like season one of Anime Club,

00:15:12.844 --> 00:15:14.311
it started a lot of,

00:15:14.311 --> 00:15:16.139
let's say, controversial opinions

00:15:16.139 --> 00:15:17.472
within the anime community.

00:15:17.472 --> 00:15:18.409
- [Reina] Right, right, right.

00:15:18.409 --> 00:15:20.272
- 'Cause I remember it was the
first time where we saw this

00:15:20.272 --> 00:15:23.181
kind of mainstream YouTuber try to, like,

00:15:23.181 --> 00:15:25.691
bring the anime culture to the
wider, mainstream audience.

00:15:25.691 --> 00:15:27.951
Nowadays, it's like pretty--

00:15:27.951 --> 00:15:29.850
- Every brand is like,
"Hey, I like anime!"

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:30.683
(all agreeing)

00:15:30.683 --> 00:15:31.516
- Everyone talks about anime now.

00:15:31.516 --> 00:15:32.439
- "We at Arby's love anime."

00:15:32.439 --> 00:15:34.372
- Back then, we had this like anime show,

00:15:34.372 --> 00:15:37.112
that was like produced by Philly D.

00:15:37.112 --> 00:15:38.909
or he had his name
involved with SourceFed,

00:15:38.909 --> 00:15:41.361
and this was like the most
mainstream anime had gotten

00:15:41.361 --> 00:15:42.360
to any YouTuber.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.836
And, you know, all my love to Meg.

00:15:45.836 --> 00:15:50.611
There was like some episodes
where some lines were said,

00:15:50.611 --> 00:15:52.932
where I can't remember if it
was her or like her guest host,

00:15:52.932 --> 00:15:57.932
where they called Full Metal
Alchemist a shoujo anime.

00:15:58.155 --> 00:15:59.902
(guys laughing)

00:15:59.902 --> 00:16:04.902
- I could feel unbridled grudge
in you as you're talking.

00:16:05.034 --> 00:16:08.082
- You remember how anime fans
were back in the day, right?

00:16:08.082 --> 00:16:12.415
They saw this medium as,
like, a fucking holy grail.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.587
- I think it didn't help, as
well, that the show itself,

00:16:15.587 --> 00:16:18.226
was probably the most well
produced out of, like,

00:16:18.226 --> 00:16:20.237
any ani-tube content.

00:16:20.237 --> 00:16:21.447
- [Reina] Of course, yeah.
- At the time.

00:16:21.447 --> 00:16:23.226
So, it just felt like, you know, like,

00:16:23.226 --> 00:16:25.986
"Yes, I too watch anime,
younger generation!"

00:16:25.986 --> 00:16:28.524
- 'Cause I felt like it wasn't her fault.

00:16:28.524 --> 00:16:30.276
It was just the fault of just,

00:16:30.276 --> 00:16:32.206
I think the way the show was set up

00:16:32.206 --> 00:16:34.177
just wasn't the right way to, like,

00:16:34.177 --> 00:16:36.186
really embrace the medium of anime

00:16:36.186 --> 00:16:37.995
or like introduce it to a new audience.

00:16:37.995 --> 00:16:39.956
But, I'll say this,

00:16:39.956 --> 00:16:41.137
like, what you and William did

00:16:41.137 --> 00:16:42.197
- Aw!

00:16:42.197 --> 00:16:44.208
- You made like,

00:16:44.208 --> 00:16:45.526
it was a huge improvement, right?

00:16:45.526 --> 00:16:49.580
Because I feel like the
chemistry was a lot better.

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:52.400
But what I wanted to ask is that,

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.967
did you, going into this,

00:16:54.967 --> 00:16:56.758
did you know about all
of the negative feedback

00:16:56.758 --> 00:16:59.062
that you had gotten
from, like, anime fans?

00:16:59.062 --> 00:17:02.743
- Um, as a translator, I
was already pretty connected

00:17:02.743 --> 00:17:04.948
to a lot of different anime sites.

00:17:04.948 --> 00:17:07.308
So, I was well aware.

00:17:07.308 --> 00:17:09.089
Well, "well aware" isn't
the right way to say it.

00:17:09.089 --> 00:17:10.100
I was aware.

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:13.531
But, putting a face to the
opinion is a whole nother world.

00:17:13.531 --> 00:17:14.759
(guys agreeing)

00:17:14.759 --> 00:17:16.782
and the backlash is quite scary.

00:17:16.782 --> 00:17:18.851
However, exactly how you said,

00:17:18.851 --> 00:17:23.080
I think people were quite
excited about the improvement.

00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:28.080
And there's a lot less you
can criticize to a translator.

00:17:28.588 --> 00:17:29.701
(guys humming in agreement)

00:17:29.701 --> 00:17:31.348
And also, I'm like actually Japanese.

00:17:31.348 --> 00:17:34.101
So, that like kind of mattered
in their eyes, as well.

00:17:34.101 --> 00:17:35.090
That's also like another creepy thing

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:35.923
about anime fans, right?

00:17:35.923 --> 00:17:37.120
Like, especially back in the day.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:39.507
There's like so much more nerd cred

00:17:39.507 --> 00:17:40.910
if you're like actually Japanese.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:42.861
That means you can watch it without subs!

00:17:42.861 --> 00:17:45.611
- Joey's like, "Is there?"

00:17:45.611 --> 00:17:47.681
- I would argue that
still exists to this day.

00:17:47.681 --> 00:17:48.750
- No, it does, it totally does.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:49.969
- Right, no, it does, it does.

00:17:49.969 --> 00:17:51.736
Yeah, I was trying to
be like modest about it.

00:17:51.736 --> 00:17:53.251
But that mattered

00:17:53.251 --> 00:17:54.531
and I think that overrode

00:17:54.531 --> 00:17:56.690
any major, major criticisms.

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:58.931
And also, again, I was
already in that sphere,

00:17:58.931 --> 00:18:01.947
so I was conscious of what
to say, what not to say.

00:18:01.947 --> 00:18:03.639
If Will said something weird,

00:18:03.639 --> 00:18:04.559
I'd go to the producer and the editor

00:18:04.559 --> 00:18:06.660
and be like, "You've got to
cut that out." (guys laughing)

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.200
- 'Cause like, I've always been curious,

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.892
because, you know, no disrespect to Meg,

00:18:10.892 --> 00:18:13.170
it felt like you did take
a lot of the criticisms

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:14.089
that they had gotten

00:18:14.089 --> 00:18:16.342
and really used that to improve the show.

00:18:16.342 --> 00:18:18.799
And I was wondering if it was,
like, a conscious decision?

00:18:18.799 --> 00:18:20.511
- Very conscious, yes.

00:18:20.511 --> 00:18:21.761
Very conscious.

00:18:22.969 --> 00:18:25.100
It really was unfair to Meg,

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:27.979
because she was the only
person in the entire building

00:18:27.979 --> 00:18:29.755
that even knew anything about anime.

00:18:29.755 --> 00:18:30.588
(guys agreeing)

00:18:30.588 --> 00:18:31.686
The editors didn't know.

00:18:31.686 --> 00:18:32.529
So, she had to source
all the photos herself.

00:18:32.529 --> 00:18:35.020
I mean, granted, I did
too, after I took over.

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:38.059
But, there was no producer that oversaw

00:18:38.059 --> 00:18:39.830
what was correct or incorrect,

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.539
or knew the sub-audience or like,

00:18:41.539 --> 00:18:43.838
knew how to, like, hunt Reddit
for it and stuff like that.

00:18:43.838 --> 00:18:47.985
So, I came in and I kind of
started from the back-log.

00:18:47.985 --> 00:18:50.589
And I was, due to the
research, I knew everything

00:18:50.589 --> 00:18:52.917
that would make sense, would maybe hit,

00:18:52.917 --> 00:18:55.799
would be bad, is Will going to understand?

00:18:55.799 --> 00:18:57.547
Like, that was also the
other tether, right?

00:18:57.547 --> 00:18:59.836
Like, you have to make sure
that the novice character

00:18:59.836 --> 00:19:03.566
is going to be able to
kind of follow it, as well,

00:19:03.566 --> 00:19:04.525
without having to use like, high level--

00:19:04.525 --> 00:19:06.592
- 'Cause, I guess, he needs
to like channel people

00:19:06.592 --> 00:19:07.792
who are watching the video that

00:19:07.792 --> 00:19:08.841
don't know anything about it.

00:19:08.841 --> 00:19:09.712
- Right, right, right.

00:19:09.712 --> 00:19:11.961
But, again, sometimes he was so random,

00:19:11.961 --> 00:19:14.092
that it was hard to be like,
"Nobody's thinking that!"

00:19:14.092 --> 00:19:15.032
(all laughing)

00:19:15.032 --> 00:19:17.230
"Nobody thinks that, don't ask that!"

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:18.611
- It sounds like before,

00:19:18.611 --> 00:19:21.141
it was a situation where,
like, the teacher's like,

00:19:21.141 --> 00:19:23.240
"Oh, that one kid's good at computers,

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:25.412
"he can fix everything, right?

00:19:25.412 --> 00:19:26.511
"Get him in."

00:19:26.511 --> 00:19:28.193
- That's exactly what it felt like.

00:19:28.193 --> 00:19:30.225
It felt like Meg just
got put in there because

00:19:30.225 --> 00:19:32.274
she was the only one that knew
anything about anime at all.

00:19:32.274 --> 00:19:33.996
- And you know it's awful because

00:19:33.996 --> 00:19:35.754
it was such a passion project of hers.

00:19:35.754 --> 00:19:37.508
And she wanted to do it as a fan,

00:19:37.508 --> 00:19:38.793
but you have to get technical.

00:19:38.793 --> 00:19:39.626
You have to.

00:19:39.626 --> 00:19:40.459
(guys agreeing)

00:19:40.459 --> 00:19:41.858
Especially if you don't have
a producer and an editor

00:19:41.858 --> 00:19:43.955
that's gonna fix it on your
behalf after you're done.

00:19:43.955 --> 00:19:46.814
Like, she wanted it to
be opinionated, and fun,

00:19:46.814 --> 00:19:51.081
and what she likes, and that
would've worked maybe now.

00:19:51.081 --> 00:19:53.866
But that was so not the case
on a platform like SourceFed.

00:19:53.866 --> 00:19:57.305
So, it just did not work at the time.

00:19:57.305 --> 00:19:58.138
- I feel like, you were like,

00:19:58.138 --> 00:20:00.495
that idea was a few
years ahead of it's time.

00:20:00.495 --> 00:20:03.935
In terms of a talk show about anime

00:20:03.935 --> 00:20:05.764
that could really do well on YouTube now.

00:20:05.764 --> 00:20:09.456
I feel like, back then, there was no way,

00:20:09.456 --> 00:20:11.325
it was really hard to get
new people into anime.

00:20:11.325 --> 00:20:13.435
Especially on YouTube.
- [Reina] I think so.

00:20:13.435 --> 00:20:14.364
- And a lot of the backlash you got

00:20:14.364 --> 00:20:16.642
were just from anime fans, as being like,

00:20:16.642 --> 00:20:18.871
"Okay, no, we don't want this to be

00:20:18.871 --> 00:20:20.894
"the representation of
what anime fans are."

00:20:20.894 --> 00:20:21.986
And now I just think,

00:20:21.986 --> 00:20:25.056
"Anime fans were pretty
fucking toxic back then,

00:20:25.056 --> 00:20:25.889
"as well, weren't they?"

00:20:25.889 --> 00:20:26.757
- Yeah.
- I mean, they still are.

00:20:26.757 --> 00:20:28.176
Love you, guys, but we are.

00:20:28.176 --> 00:20:29.059
Love you, guys.

00:20:29.059 --> 00:20:31.606
- But I feel like early YouTube,
they were like next level.

00:20:31.606 --> 00:20:32.635
- Well, yeah, 'cause
I think at that point,

00:20:32.635 --> 00:20:33.627
again, it was like god saying,

00:20:33.627 --> 00:20:34.475
or it was like the holy grail,

00:20:34.475 --> 00:20:35.607
it was like this protected thing,

00:20:35.607 --> 00:20:36.669
where it was like it was theirs

00:20:36.669 --> 00:20:37.866
and it had been like tarnished.

00:20:37.866 --> 00:20:39.056
- I kind of understand that, though.

00:20:39.056 --> 00:20:40.255
Like, when I was younger

00:20:40.255 --> 00:20:43.708
and I was in Japanese
school in New Jersey,

00:20:43.708 --> 00:20:45.867
when Pokemon came to the States,

00:20:45.867 --> 00:20:47.817
I was like, "That's ours!"

00:20:47.817 --> 00:20:48.856
(guys laughing)

00:20:48.856 --> 00:20:50.549
Like, "What are you doing?!"

00:20:50.549 --> 00:20:51.597
- How dare you?!

00:20:51.597 --> 00:20:52.487
- His name is Ash?!

00:20:52.487 --> 00:20:53.950
Like, you know what I mean?

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:54.896
(guys agreeing)

00:20:54.896 --> 00:20:56.327
Like, even as a kid I kind of understood

00:20:56.327 --> 00:20:57.506
that exclusivity thing.

00:20:57.506 --> 00:21:01.168
Also, like, as an anime fan,
I understood anime fans.

00:21:01.168 --> 00:21:04.625
And I actually, surprisingly,
didn't get as much hate

00:21:04.625 --> 00:21:06.976
especially throughout SourceFed.

00:21:06.976 --> 00:21:09.287
They were actually very
happy that I existed.

00:21:09.287 --> 00:21:11.197
So, I was super lucky there.

00:21:11.197 --> 00:21:13.584
Got a lot of hate on Crunchyroll. (laughs)

00:21:13.584 --> 00:21:15.326
- I think it was just
because it was so obvious

00:21:15.326 --> 00:21:16.876
that you were just a
weeb through and through.

00:21:16.876 --> 00:21:17.906
- Yeah.

00:21:17.906 --> 00:21:19.895
- People just saw you
and they were just like,

00:21:19.895 --> 00:21:21.146
"Okay, she's one of us now."

00:21:21.146 --> 00:21:23.405
- I got accepted, yes.

00:21:23.405 --> 00:21:25.128
- You weren't trying to
pretend to be a weeb,

00:21:25.128 --> 00:21:25.961
you were a weeb.

00:21:25.961 --> 00:21:28.795
- A lot of the time with these,
you know, company videos,

00:21:28.795 --> 00:21:30.095
so much of it is like,

00:21:30.095 --> 00:21:32.358
"Hi, guys. We love the
thing that you love, right?

00:21:32.358 --> 00:21:33.566
"It's kind of wacky, huh?

00:21:33.566 --> 00:21:35.115
"This thing's wacky, too?

00:21:35.115 --> 00:21:37.249
"Oh, you guys, this is so wacky."

00:21:37.249 --> 00:21:39.008
- "FMA, my favorite shoujo!"

00:21:39.008 --> 00:21:43.258
- "FML, this was a cringe
moment, right guys? Ugh!"

00:21:44.551 --> 00:21:45.886
- I wanted to give people
the benefit of the doubt

00:21:45.886 --> 00:21:48.827
back then, and there was like that thing

00:21:48.827 --> 00:21:50.745
that was in one show,

00:21:50.745 --> 00:21:52.939
and I think the other thing,

00:21:52.939 --> 00:21:54.573
one of them was talking about Baccano,

00:21:54.573 --> 00:21:57.613
and I think the guy that
was new there, he said,

00:21:57.613 --> 00:22:01.523
"Oh, it kind of feels like
a rip off to FMA, right?"

00:22:01.523 --> 00:22:02.356
(Connor laughing loudly)

00:22:02.356 --> 00:22:04.503
"'Cause Baccano's set on a train

00:22:04.503 --> 00:22:06.732
"and there's an episode of
FMA that's set on a train."

00:22:06.732 --> 00:22:08.023
(guys laughing)

00:22:08.023 --> 00:22:09.370
and even that, that was like

00:22:09.370 --> 00:22:12.098
the space bar moment for me, as well.

00:22:12.098 --> 00:22:13.098
- Oh my god.

00:22:14.303 --> 00:22:16.192
- And I just think you did such a good job

00:22:16.192 --> 00:22:19.102
because this is the
environment that you came into

00:22:19.102 --> 00:22:20.823
to have to prove people that,

00:22:20.823 --> 00:22:22.803
okay there is an actual big
improvement to this show

00:22:22.803 --> 00:22:23.873
and you did a great job.

00:22:23.873 --> 00:22:25.262
- Does that video still exist on YouTube?

00:22:25.262 --> 00:22:26.095
- Sorry?

00:22:26.095 --> 00:22:27.183
- Does that video still exist on YouTube?

00:22:27.183 --> 00:22:28.016
- I think season one of Anime Club

00:22:28.016 --> 00:22:30.373
- We've got to find this.
- is not on YouTube anymore.

00:22:30.373 --> 00:22:31.373
- That's like being like,

00:22:31.373 --> 00:22:33.956
"So, like, Train to Busan
is a little bit of a rip off

00:22:33.956 --> 00:22:36.240
"of Hogwarts because they're
both on a train, right?"

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.073
- So, unfortunately, you're just going to

00:22:37.073 --> 00:22:37.906
have to take my word,

00:22:37.906 --> 00:22:39.872
but anyone who watched SourceFed

00:22:39.872 --> 00:22:41.763
back in the day, in the anime community,

00:22:41.763 --> 00:22:43.412
knows that, that was like shared around.

00:22:43.412 --> 00:22:44.292
- Yeah, yeah.

00:22:44.292 --> 00:22:46.251
- Right, so I'm trying to pull
up the timeline in my head.

00:22:46.251 --> 00:22:48.474
So, you're in SourceFed for how long?

00:22:48.474 --> 00:22:50.921
- I was there for, damn.

00:22:50.921 --> 00:22:54.343
Let's see, I was an intern
first, which was 2012.

00:22:54.343 --> 00:22:57.197
I was a actual host in 2014.

00:22:57.197 --> 00:23:01.209
I was there for two years, no,
I was there for three years.

00:23:01.209 --> 00:23:02.918
But it felt like much longer.

00:23:02.918 --> 00:23:03.912
- Well, it sounds like

00:23:03.912 --> 00:23:05.593
a really important part
of, like, the career.

00:23:05.593 --> 00:23:06.513
- Absolutely! Yeah!

00:23:06.513 --> 00:23:09.343
And I also did a lot of
things with them afterwards.

00:23:09.343 --> 00:23:12.100
So, it kind of really did
feel much longer than that.

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:13.232
(guys agreeing)

00:23:13.232 --> 00:23:15.351
- And so, the reason why
you left is obviously,

00:23:15.351 --> 00:23:16.580
'cause did it just get wiped?

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:18.830
- Well, I left before that.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:19.879
- Oh, okay, okay.

00:23:19.879 --> 00:23:21.742
- Yeah, so this is such a strange thing.

00:23:21.742 --> 00:23:22.822
I never talked about it before,

00:23:22.822 --> 00:23:26.702
but my everything went
into our gaming channel.

00:23:26.702 --> 00:23:27.991
Me and Steven Suptic's.

00:23:27.991 --> 00:23:29.328
And we were doing so well!

00:23:29.328 --> 00:23:30.571
And then they canceled it anyway.

00:23:30.571 --> 00:23:32.441
And I was so heartbroken.

00:23:32.441 --> 00:23:33.520
- Did they say why?

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.342
- They just said that
there was no budget for it.

00:23:36.342 --> 00:23:37.277
Which is such a, I know.

00:23:37.277 --> 00:23:38.258
- To let play?

00:23:38.258 --> 00:23:39.091
Like, what?

00:23:39.091 --> 00:23:40.828
Like, they buy the game on Steam.

00:23:40.828 --> 00:23:42.031
- Yeah, I know!

00:23:42.031 --> 00:23:43.037
It's crazy.

00:23:43.037 --> 00:23:44.187
- We don't have $10 for the Steam game.

00:23:44.187 --> 00:23:47.528
- But I must say, Suptic
did spend over two grand

00:23:47.528 --> 00:23:49.266
on CS Go cases once.

00:23:49.266 --> 00:23:50.198
(guys laughing)

00:23:50.198 --> 00:23:52.308
Using the company card and we wrote it off

00:23:52.308 --> 00:23:53.518
and it was like okayed.

00:23:53.518 --> 00:23:54.648
That's their fault!

00:23:54.648 --> 00:23:56.096
They should've just
told us it wasn't okay!

00:23:56.096 --> 00:23:56.929
- That is hilarious.

00:23:56.929 --> 00:23:58.769
That is such an epic gamer move.

00:23:58.769 --> 00:23:59.666
- Yeah, I know.

00:23:59.666 --> 00:24:03.268
And he got, like, the
fuckin' butterfly knife.

00:24:03.268 --> 00:24:05.329
(guys laughing and cheering)

00:24:05.329 --> 00:24:06.625
So, like, whatever!

00:24:06.625 --> 00:24:08.069
But anyway (laughs)

00:24:08.069 --> 00:24:10.106
Well yeah, they said there was no budget.

00:24:10.106 --> 00:24:11.815
And there's like a lot of, like,

00:24:11.815 --> 00:24:13.422
production reasons behind it.

00:24:13.422 --> 00:24:17.946
But then because the whole
channel was dissolved,

00:24:17.946 --> 00:24:19.529
and I was a OG host

00:24:21.583 --> 00:24:23.353
for that time,

00:24:23.353 --> 00:24:26.205
they brought me back into SourceFedNERD,

00:24:26.205 --> 00:24:28.084
and SourceFed Proper instead of, like,

00:24:28.084 --> 00:24:29.765
outright laying me off.

00:24:29.765 --> 00:24:31.875
But they laid off Suptic

00:24:31.875 --> 00:24:34.610
and I was like so weird about that,

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:37.036
but I also needed money to survive in LA.

00:24:37.036 --> 00:24:38.905
So, there was not much
I could do about it.

00:24:38.905 --> 00:24:43.238
And then, when I left,
Suptic got hired again

00:24:43.238 --> 00:24:46.791
as, like, a SourceFed Proper member.

00:24:46.791 --> 00:24:47.849
- [Garnt] What?

00:24:47.849 --> 00:24:48.941
- I know, I know, so strange.

00:24:48.941 --> 00:24:51.301
Yeah, and that's when I kind of realized

00:24:51.301 --> 00:24:52.993
that something was super, super wrong

00:24:52.993 --> 00:24:54.082
with the entirety of the company.

00:24:54.082 --> 00:24:55.574
Not that there's anything
wrong with Steve!

00:24:55.574 --> 00:24:56.932
But you know the fact that like--

00:24:56.932 --> 00:24:57.974
- Letting someone go and then

00:24:57.974 --> 00:24:59.645
bringing them back in
later is really bizarre.

00:24:59.645 --> 00:25:01.403
- Yeah, and then it almost felt like

00:25:01.403 --> 00:25:03.155
they were trying separate us

00:25:03.155 --> 00:25:04.934
so that we wouldn't try
something on our own,

00:25:04.934 --> 00:25:05.854
but then we did.

00:25:05.854 --> 00:25:07.834
(guys agreeing) But then we
did, so that didn't work.

00:25:07.834 --> 00:25:10.381
But yeah, that was like a major thing.

00:25:10.381 --> 00:25:13.384
Also, you know, Discovery,
like, owned our face and soul

00:25:13.384 --> 00:25:15.465
for X amount of years,
according to our contract.

00:25:15.465 --> 00:25:16.675
'Cause we were an ensemble cast.

00:25:16.675 --> 00:25:17.955
- [Garnt] Oh god, oh god.

00:25:17.955 --> 00:25:19.914
- So that was also, I'm
suspecting that was also

00:25:19.914 --> 00:25:22.016
why they maybe wanted to keep us separate,

00:25:22.016 --> 00:25:24.605
so that our faces wouldn't
be together on another thing

00:25:24.605 --> 00:25:25.781
that we created on our own.

00:25:25.781 --> 00:25:28.236
- Because you and Steve
created like a brand,

00:25:28.236 --> 00:25:30.174
almost created like a brand for your own.

00:25:30.174 --> 00:25:32.532
- Yeah, we ended up,
like, kind of doing that.

00:25:32.532 --> 00:25:34.845
Which was amazing, but
also, I do think that,

00:25:34.845 --> 00:25:37.164
that probably was
dangerous for the company

00:25:37.164 --> 00:25:38.885
and for the shareholders maybe.

00:25:38.885 --> 00:25:41.526
- 'Cause the thing about, you know,

00:25:41.526 --> 00:25:45.302
big companies hiring YouTubers
to do videos for the company,

00:25:45.302 --> 00:25:47.894
is that the audience end
up being loyal to the host

00:25:47.894 --> 00:25:49.006
and the personality,

00:25:49.006 --> 00:25:51.276
because they're the heart
and soul of the show.

00:25:51.276 --> 00:25:52.593
So, even if they leave,

00:25:52.593 --> 00:25:56.670
you've already created a
brand for yourself, right?

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:57.503
And yeah, I mean,

00:25:57.503 --> 00:26:02.253
it fucking is pretty scummy
that stuff like that happens.

00:26:03.684 --> 00:26:06.145
- It sucks that, like, now
having been in the industry

00:26:06.145 --> 00:26:08.430
in many different ways, like
as an independent YouTuber,

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:10.133
as somebody that's hired
by bigger companies,

00:26:10.133 --> 00:26:14.484
like, it's entertainment,
media, new media, old media,

00:26:14.484 --> 00:26:16.722
it kind of be helped right now.

00:26:16.722 --> 00:26:18.429
I think it's starting to improve

00:26:18.429 --> 00:26:20.152
or maybe change in a way

00:26:20.152 --> 00:26:21.424
that's beneficial to independent creators.

00:26:21.424 --> 00:26:24.274
Especially with YouTube and
Twitch and everything like that.

00:26:24.274 --> 00:26:26.753
But, it's still very difficult.

00:26:26.753 --> 00:26:29.766
Like, the biggest chunk
of money that's solid

00:26:29.766 --> 00:26:32.604
is definitely still in the
hands of bigger companies.

00:26:32.604 --> 00:26:35.216
So, that does definitely kind of sway.

00:26:35.216 --> 00:26:37.094
- Yeah, and what I wanted to know is,

00:26:37.094 --> 00:26:39.392
you've obviously got involved in this

00:26:39.392 --> 00:26:41.143
by being an intern for Philly D.

00:26:41.143 --> 00:26:43.404
Is that the job that you wanted?

00:26:43.404 --> 00:26:45.393
Like, did you want to work in YouTube?

00:26:45.393 --> 00:26:46.893
- Um, I was a fan.

00:26:47.872 --> 00:26:49.192
And I was just watching him

00:26:49.192 --> 00:26:52.183
and I was watching some of SourceFed

00:26:52.183 --> 00:26:55.014
and I was, what was I doing?

00:26:55.014 --> 00:26:58.092
Oh yeah, I was an apprentice
pastry chef in New Jersey.

00:26:58.092 --> 00:26:59.364
(guys laughing in disbelief)

00:26:59.364 --> 00:27:00.398
Yeah, I know, I know.

00:27:00.398 --> 00:27:01.749
After having graduated from college.

00:27:01.749 --> 00:27:02.810
- Is that what you wanted to do though?

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:03.860
- Um...

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:06.168
This is so weird, I knew I was good at it.

00:27:06.168 --> 00:27:07.157
Sounds so pretentious.

