- Iron Mouse is crazy, she's great though.

00:43:20.263 --> 00:43:21.918
- Well, is there anything you'd, like,

00:43:21.918 --> 00:43:23.391
want to do if you start doing Twitch?

00:43:23.391 --> 00:43:25.167
Like, do you want to play games?

00:43:25.167 --> 00:43:26.954
Do you want to just do,
like, a just chatting?

00:43:26.954 --> 00:43:29.433
- You know, I miss playing games.

00:43:29.433 --> 00:43:31.247
I miss playing games, like,

00:43:31.247 --> 00:43:32.890
for the purpose of being on camera.

00:43:32.890 --> 00:43:34.580
So, probably fifty fifty.

00:43:34.580 --> 00:43:36.737
- Reina Scully streams, coming soon.

00:43:36.737 --> 00:43:38.654
- Oh my god, thank you!

00:43:39.728 --> 00:43:43.027
Yeah, I think I just
want a different platform

00:43:43.027 --> 00:43:44.626
than YouTube, right now.

00:43:44.626 --> 00:43:45.572
That's really what it is.

00:43:45.572 --> 00:43:49.234
I'm a little bit sick of
making YouTube videos.

00:43:49.234 --> 00:43:52.866
We all feel it, don't be
like, "Oh, no!" (laughs)

00:43:52.866 --> 00:43:54.545
- (sarcastically) No, I love it!

00:43:54.545 --> 00:43:58.150
- It depends on how my
morning goes, you know.

00:43:58.150 --> 00:43:59.033
- I feel that.

00:43:59.033 --> 00:44:00.155
- Some days I'm like, "Fuck,
I don't want to do this."

00:44:00.155 --> 00:44:02.254
- It also depends on the YouTube idea.

00:44:02.254 --> 00:44:03.905
Some of them are literally just like,

00:44:03.905 --> 00:44:05.855
"Okay, here's the grind. I'm
going to just grind it out."

00:44:05.855 --> 00:44:07.801
Some projects you're excited for.

00:44:07.801 --> 00:44:09.051
But it very much depends.

00:44:09.051 --> 00:44:10.934
- Totally! Yeah, I think
it really is just, like,

00:44:10.934 --> 00:44:12.594
a pandemic world thing.

00:44:12.594 --> 00:44:15.851
I used to make a lot of videos
going out and going to Tokyo,

00:44:15.851 --> 00:44:17.800
hanging out with Joey, like
doing a bunch of things.

00:44:17.800 --> 00:44:19.460
And like with my friends in Tokyo.

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:22.342
And I don't like streaming from,

00:44:22.342 --> 00:44:23.206
oh, not streaming,

00:44:23.206 --> 00:44:24.943
I don't like shooting
from my house, because A

00:44:24.943 --> 00:44:29.123
I don't want to accidentally
reveal where I am exactly.

00:44:29.123 --> 00:44:31.282
Like, I'm in the inaka,
I'm in the countryside.

00:44:31.282 --> 00:44:34.197
So, it's a little bit hard
to figure out where I am.

00:44:34.197 --> 00:44:36.230
But, like, just in case,
it's a little bit freaky.

00:44:36.230 --> 00:44:39.191
And, you know, I guess your inspiration

00:44:39.191 --> 00:44:40.901
is kinda limited when you're just

00:44:40.901 --> 00:44:41.984
doing it from home, by yourself.

00:44:41.984 --> 00:44:43.096
- I mean, yeah, someone got very close to

00:44:43.096 --> 00:44:44.778
finding where I live right now.

00:44:44.778 --> 00:44:46.399
- Oh, what?
- Did I tell you this?

00:44:46.399 --> 00:44:47.232
- No.
- No.

00:44:47.232 --> 00:44:48.926
- So, there was, like, a bar code

00:44:48.926 --> 00:44:51.417
that was roughly shown for, like, a second

00:44:51.417 --> 00:44:53.458
in one of my videos, from
one of the packages I opened.

00:44:53.458 --> 00:44:54.291
- Right.

00:44:54.291 --> 00:44:56.480
- And they went around, texting people,

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:58.141
being like, "I know where Connor lives."

00:44:58.141 --> 00:44:58.977
(Reina gasping)

00:44:58.977 --> 00:45:01.543
And then, so, what happened was, was that

00:45:01.543 --> 00:45:03.538
even though this bar
code was barely visible,

00:45:03.538 --> 00:45:05.728
somehow, like, it's in the background,

00:45:05.728 --> 00:45:07.945
and somehow it scanned.

00:45:07.945 --> 00:45:08.903
- [Joey] What?!

00:45:08.903 --> 00:45:09.736
- What?!

00:45:09.736 --> 00:45:10.569
- When you put the scanner to it.

00:45:10.569 --> 00:45:11.402
- That's what you meant?

00:45:11.402 --> 00:45:12.235
I didn't understand at first.

00:45:12.235 --> 00:45:13.230
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:14.207
So, like, when you get a Japanese package,

00:45:14.207 --> 00:45:15.133
I think any package,

00:45:15.133 --> 00:45:16.407
there's always a bar code on it.

00:45:16.407 --> 00:45:17.240
(group agreeing)

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:18.707
And this was, like, in the
background of the shot.

00:45:18.707 --> 00:45:20.557
And someone, somehow scanned it.

00:45:20.557 --> 00:45:23.277
I have no idea how they got,
like, the fidelity high enough.

00:45:23.277 --> 00:45:24.110
- Enhance, enhance!

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:25.776
(all laughing)
- That's what I was thinking!

00:45:25.776 --> 00:45:27.258
They did the enhancements.

00:45:27.258 --> 00:45:28.902
- I found where it was,

00:45:28.902 --> 00:45:31.267
it didn't even work when I was doing it.

00:45:31.267 --> 00:45:33.990
So, I don't understand how
they managed to pull it off.

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:35.351
'Cause then they were going around,

00:45:35.351 --> 00:45:36.313
sending me a screenshot like,

00:45:36.313 --> 00:45:37.427
"I know where you live, Connor."

00:45:37.427 --> 00:45:39.409
And then, so, I looked at the thing

00:45:39.409 --> 00:45:42.175
and, luckily, Japan Post
only told my post code.

00:45:42.175 --> 00:45:44.417
Which is still not good,
but I was like, "All right."

00:45:44.417 --> 00:45:46.030
- It's only a post code.

00:45:46.030 --> 00:45:47.739
- That's close, though.

00:45:47.739 --> 00:45:49.385
- Yeah, I was like, "All
right, what are you gonna do?

00:45:49.385 --> 00:45:51.029
"come to this giant-ass place,

00:45:51.029 --> 00:45:53.099
"with all these, like,
ten story apartments?"

00:45:53.099 --> 00:45:54.765
- And knock on every door.
- Yeah, I'm like,

00:45:54.765 --> 00:45:55.694
"What are you gonna
do, find my apartment?"

00:45:55.694 --> 00:45:56.965
I'm like, I would gamble

00:45:56.965 --> 00:45:59.075
even if you found a building I lived in.

00:45:59.075 --> 00:46:00.710
I'd still feel safe, honestly.

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:02.378
I'm like, "Try and find the door."

00:46:02.378 --> 00:46:03.211
Don't try and find the
door, that was a joke.

00:46:03.211 --> 00:46:04.044
- Oh my god.

00:46:04.044 --> 00:46:05.458
- But, like, I mean, you know.

00:46:05.458 --> 00:46:06.520
It was very concerning.

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:08.145
You should be careful with bar codes.

00:46:08.145 --> 00:46:10.360
- Yeah, yeah, I had no
idea that was a thing.

00:46:10.360 --> 00:46:12.650
- Fuck that person
going around being like,

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:14.731
"This is so cool, I have
Connor's address, I think."

00:46:14.731 --> 00:46:15.842
- Don't be like that.

00:46:15.842 --> 00:46:17.028
Don't do that.

00:46:17.028 --> 00:46:17.861
- But it's whatever.

00:46:17.861 --> 00:46:19.831
I mean, I was like, "All
right, cool, you're a loser."

00:46:19.831 --> 00:46:22.113
Why would you see a bar code in the video

00:46:22.113 --> 00:46:23.538
and think, "I'm gonna scan that.

00:46:23.538 --> 00:46:24.660
"I'm gonna fuckin' try and scan that."

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:26.312
- They love you. (laughs)

00:46:26.312 --> 00:46:29.238
- I love him so much I must
find out where he lives.

00:46:29.238 --> 00:46:31.603
- I must dox him, that's
how much I love him.

00:46:31.603 --> 00:46:32.439
- Yeah!

00:46:32.439 --> 00:46:34.102
- I probably have
cockroaches in my apartment,

00:46:34.102 --> 00:46:35.785
why would you want to
find out where I live?

00:46:35.785 --> 00:46:36.726
I don't know.

00:46:36.726 --> 00:46:38.110
- 'Cause he loves you
and your cockroaches.

00:46:38.110 --> 00:46:39.032
(Connor chuckling)

00:46:39.032 --> 00:46:40.365
- Everything about you, all of you.

00:46:40.365 --> 00:46:41.198
- Oh god.

00:46:41.198 --> 00:46:42.031
(Reina laughing)

00:46:42.031 --> 00:46:42.867
- That's so fucking creepy.

00:46:42.867 --> 00:46:43.777
- Yeah, it was.
- Yeah, that sucks.

00:46:43.777 --> 00:46:45.725
- Yeah, but I mean, where you live though,

00:46:45.725 --> 00:46:47.416
it's probably really safe though.

00:46:47.416 --> 00:46:48.565
- It is!

00:46:48.565 --> 00:46:51.065
Except we just had a stabbing.

00:46:52.073 --> 00:46:52.906
- What?!

00:46:52.906 --> 00:46:53.858
- First one in a hundred years or?

00:46:53.858 --> 00:46:55.024
- That's what I'm saying!

00:46:55.024 --> 00:46:57.011
It never happens, so it
freaked everybody out.

00:46:57.011 --> 00:46:57.847
But yeah!

00:46:57.847 --> 00:46:59.662
- Was this, like, national news?

00:46:59.662 --> 00:47:02.924
- Um, it was definitely, like--

00:47:02.924 --> 00:47:03.771
- Did they die?

00:47:03.771 --> 00:47:05.128
- Yeah.

00:47:05.128 --> 00:47:06.603
- Oh shit, I thought, fuck.

00:47:06.603 --> 00:47:08.417
- Shizuoka news, yeah.

00:47:08.417 --> 00:47:10.921
And, it makes no sense, but
then, a whole week later,

00:47:10.921 --> 00:47:13.372
he didn't get caught and everybody forgot!

00:47:13.372 --> 00:47:14.549
So, there's that.

00:47:14.549 --> 00:47:15.576
- Well!

00:47:15.576 --> 00:47:16.409
- Okay.

00:47:16.409 --> 00:47:19.402
- But that's a misnomer, that's
not a good representation

00:47:19.402 --> 00:47:22.786
of my hometown, it's very
lovely and quaint and wonderful.

00:47:22.786 --> 00:47:24.429
- That's like where I grew up, though.

00:47:24.429 --> 00:47:26.122
I remember when someone
died when I was like 18,

00:47:26.122 --> 00:47:27.673
they were like, "Yeah
this is the first death

00:47:27.673 --> 00:47:29.043
"in this area in like 25 years."

00:47:29.043 --> 00:47:29.876
Like, murder.

00:47:29.876 --> 00:47:30.709
- [Joey] Jesus.

00:47:30.709 --> 00:47:32.785
- It's a big deal when it
normally doesn't happen.

00:47:32.785 --> 00:47:34.113
- Yeah, in like tiny towns, yeah.

00:47:34.113 --> 00:47:35.338
- But that's the thing with like,

00:47:35.338 --> 00:47:36.568
especially like Japan, right?

00:47:36.568 --> 00:47:38.262
It's like, there's
either no crime or, like,

00:47:38.262 --> 00:47:39.157
fuckin'--

00:47:39.157 --> 00:47:39.990
- Fucked!

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:40.875
- Like World War III.

00:47:40.875 --> 00:47:42.476
- [Reina] Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:47:42.476 --> 00:47:44.580
- It's definitely, there's
no, like, medium anywhere.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:47.295
But I'm also, you know, I
lived in LA for like 8 years.

00:47:47.295 --> 00:47:49.905
Where everybody gets shot
everywhere, at all times.

00:47:49.905 --> 00:47:51.830
- I remember the first time--

00:47:51.830 --> 00:47:53.194
- You're like, "Someone got stabbed?

00:47:53.194 --> 00:47:54.084
"That's never happened in LA!"

00:47:54.084 --> 00:47:55.151
(all laughing)

00:47:55.151 --> 00:47:56.600
- I remember the first time I went to LA,

00:47:56.600 --> 00:47:58.316
it was the first Anime Expo I went to.

00:47:58.316 --> 00:47:59.149
- Ugh!

00:47:59.149 --> 00:48:01.692
- And I remember, like,
first morning I woke up,

00:48:01.692 --> 00:48:02.934
in the hotel, right?

00:48:02.934 --> 00:48:05.366
And it's one of those, like, radio clocks.

00:48:05.366 --> 00:48:06.695
So, like, the alarm goes off

00:48:06.695 --> 00:48:09.019
and then the radio starts playing, right?

00:48:09.019 --> 00:48:10.270
- As the alarm.

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:11.516
- Yeah, as the alarm, right?

00:48:11.516 --> 00:48:13.473
And I was, like, I thought
that was really cool,

00:48:13.473 --> 00:48:14.957
because, like, I'd only
ever seen that in movies.

00:48:14.957 --> 00:48:16.001
- [Reina] Oh my.

00:48:16.001 --> 00:48:17.894
- So, I was, like, "Okay, you know what?

00:48:17.894 --> 00:48:19.829
"This is a feature, I'm just gonna do it

00:48:19.829 --> 00:48:20.662
"for this one morning,

00:48:20.662 --> 00:48:23.310
"and see if it's like cool
or anything." You know?

00:48:23.310 --> 00:48:24.287
'Cause I don't know,

00:48:24.287 --> 00:48:25.622
I never had anything like
that in Australia, right?

00:48:25.622 --> 00:48:26.809
Or Japan.

00:48:26.809 --> 00:48:28.061
So, I was like, "This might be cool."

00:48:28.061 --> 00:48:30.459
So, like, alarm goes off
at, like, 8AM or something.

00:48:30.459 --> 00:48:31.384
Radio starts up.

00:48:31.384 --> 00:48:33.772
"It's a beautiful morning,
here in Los Angeles.

00:48:33.772 --> 00:48:36.742
"Last night we had three
shootings happening

00:48:36.742 --> 00:48:38.150
"down in the downtown area.

00:48:38.150 --> 00:48:40.363
"But it's a lovely summer day today!

00:48:40.363 --> 00:48:41.625
"Have a good one, everyone!"

00:48:41.625 --> 00:48:43.702
And I was like, "All right,
I'm turning that off!"

00:48:43.702 --> 00:48:45.736
- [Reina] Suspect is still at large, yeah!

00:48:45.736 --> 00:48:47.150
- I don't want to go outside right now!

00:48:47.150 --> 00:48:49.100
- Mind the helicopters
finding the suspects

00:48:49.100 --> 00:48:50.026
that are still at large!

00:48:50.026 --> 00:48:50.948
- [Joey] Exactly!

00:48:50.948 --> 00:48:51.781
- Yeah, that happened.

00:48:51.781 --> 00:48:52.614
That's a real thing.

00:48:52.614 --> 00:48:53.810
There's always a helicopter

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:57.111
shining like spotlights down
everywhere, at all times.

00:48:57.111 --> 00:48:58.471
But it was almost pretty.

00:48:58.471 --> 00:48:59.684
- Just trying to go to, like, 7-Eleven?

00:48:59.684 --> 00:49:00.793
You get your own personal spotlight?

00:49:00.793 --> 00:49:02.050
- You don't go to 7-Eleven in LA.

00:49:02.050 --> 00:49:03.248
(guys laughing)

00:49:03.248 --> 00:49:04.914
You don't do that.
- That's where it all happens!

00:49:04.914 --> 00:49:06.054
- Yeah, that's where it happens!

00:49:06.054 --> 00:49:08.218
Don't you know?
- But I want a Big Gulp, geez!

00:49:08.218 --> 00:49:09.330
(all laughing)

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:13.465
- It'll be the last thing you ever drink!

00:49:13.465 --> 00:49:14.974
- It's Slurpee Thursday!

00:49:14.974 --> 00:49:16.437
(all laughing)

00:49:16.437 --> 00:49:17.569
- Is that a thing?

00:49:17.569 --> 00:49:18.978
- I think it's something like that, right?

00:49:18.978 --> 00:49:20.412
- Thirsty Thursday, oh I don't, is it?

00:49:20.412 --> 00:49:22.043
- Thirsty Thursday or something like that.

00:49:22.043 --> 00:49:23.321
- Well, Thirsty Thursday's
like a college thing

00:49:23.321 --> 00:49:26.246
where everybody gets fucked up on Thursday

00:49:26.246 --> 00:49:28.941
and nobody goes to class on Friday.

00:49:28.941 --> 00:49:32.434
- So, going back to
the whole career thing,

00:49:32.434 --> 00:49:33.384
what happened after Crunchyroll?

00:49:33.384 --> 00:49:34.246
I'm curious.

00:49:34.246 --> 00:49:35.653
- Um, after Crunchyroll--

00:49:35.653 --> 00:49:37.059
- Is that when you started
your own YouTube channel?

00:49:37.059 --> 00:49:40.342
- Actually, I was kind of
doing stuff on SourceFed

00:49:40.342 --> 00:49:43.221
because I felt like it was such a waste

00:49:43.221 --> 00:49:45.262
to not be doing something on my own.

00:49:45.262 --> 00:49:47.041
And in my head, I was like,

00:49:47.041 --> 00:49:48.063
"Eventually, if I leave, like,

00:49:48.063 --> 00:49:48.948
"I'll have something to fall back on."

00:49:48.948 --> 00:49:50.726
- I think everyone at
SourceFed already had, like,

00:49:50.726 --> 00:49:51.560
their own main channels.

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:53.287
- Yeah, yeah, they did.

00:49:53.287 --> 00:49:55.081
We were encouraged to
do it to, kind of, like,

00:49:55.081 --> 00:49:59.445
also solidify your brand
as an on-camera host.

00:49:59.445 --> 00:50:02.029
- So, they were helping
you build up a, like,

00:50:02.029 --> 00:50:03.779
way to leave them?

00:50:03.779 --> 00:50:04.612
(guys laughing)

00:50:04.612 --> 00:50:05.822
- Well, I don't think they realized it,

00:50:05.822 --> 00:50:07.741
'cause we actually had
to sign over our channel,

00:50:07.741 --> 00:50:08.771
in the beginning.

00:50:08.771 --> 00:50:09.709
- Your personal channels?

00:50:09.709 --> 00:50:11.041
- Yeah, we had to sign it over.

00:50:11.041 --> 00:50:13.022
- Whoa!
- So, they owned that as well?

00:50:13.022 --> 00:50:14.749
- Just for, like, a normal paying job?

00:50:14.749 --> 00:50:17.420
Like, did you get any
like buy-out fee for that?

00:50:17.420 --> 00:50:18.253
- No.

00:50:18.253 --> 00:50:19.690
(guys groaning)

00:50:19.690 --> 00:50:21.430
I know. Dark, I know.

00:50:21.430 --> 00:50:23.430
But, I don't think I was

00:50:24.571 --> 00:50:26.868
a monetizable partner channel

00:50:26.868 --> 00:50:29.914
until, like, six months into SourceFed.

00:50:29.914 --> 00:50:32.906
And then I started caring that, like,

00:50:32.906 --> 00:50:33.926
they were taking my money.

00:50:33.926 --> 00:50:36.371
- I signed it off, fuck!

00:50:36.371 --> 00:50:37.644
- I was like, "They're taking my money!"

00:50:37.644 --> 00:50:39.875
But, also, monetization was
different back in the day.

00:50:39.875 --> 00:50:41.643
There were no mid-rules, right?

00:50:41.643 --> 00:50:44.846
So, there was only like a pre-roll--

00:50:44.846 --> 00:50:47.331
- But also the money
was way more, the CPM.

00:50:47.331 --> 00:50:49.118
- CPM was way higher, yeah.
- Massive.

00:50:49.118 --> 00:50:50.059
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:50:50.059 --> 00:50:52.017
So, I was not happy.

00:50:52.017 --> 00:50:54.871
But, because, like, you know,

00:50:54.871 --> 00:50:58.830
I can't do what I did what
I did on Super Panic Frenzy

00:50:58.830 --> 00:51:01.030
on my own, I wouldn't
be able to hire Suptic.

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:02.269
Like, that wouldn't be able to happen.

00:51:02.269 --> 00:51:03.589
I didn't have editors.

00:51:03.589 --> 00:51:06.237
So, like, I knew kind of
what the trade off was.

00:51:06.237 --> 00:51:07.598
And I was happy to be doing so well.

00:51:07.598 --> 00:51:11.311
So, the security in being a
hired host is much better.

00:51:11.311 --> 00:51:13.058
So, I didn't mind.

00:51:13.058 --> 00:51:15.438
Especially because I wouldn't
be able to start my channel,

00:51:15.438 --> 00:51:17.918
I think, from nothing without SourceFed.

00:51:17.918 --> 00:51:18.884
Very aware of that.

00:51:18.884 --> 00:51:20.467
- I think you could.

00:51:20.467 --> 00:51:21.300
- So nice!

00:51:21.300 --> 00:51:22.893
- No one thinks they can, right?

