weeks of summer vacation.

00:59:46.040 --> 00:59:47.970
But like, winter, there's no school.

00:59:47.970 --> 00:59:49.749
- So, when you were watching
like Phineas and Ferb,

00:59:49.749 --> 00:59:51.128
right, and they're like talking about

00:59:51.128 --> 00:59:54.666
all these days of vacation,
you go, "That's a fucking lie."

00:59:54.666 --> 00:59:55.499
- It was heartbreaking,

00:59:55.499 --> 00:59:57.560
'cause like, I'd watch
all these other cartoons

00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:59.247
that were like, "Aw, summer vacation"

00:59:59.247 --> 01:00:00.080
- Three months!

01:00:00.080 --> 01:00:01.034
- "Summer camp!" Blah, blah, blah.

01:00:01.034 --> 01:00:02.148
And I'm like, "What is that?

01:00:02.148 --> 01:00:03.948
"God, what is that?!"

01:00:03.948 --> 01:00:04.989
- "Who has time to camp

01:00:04.989 --> 01:00:06.261
"when I have to finish my math homework!"

01:00:06.261 --> 01:00:08.069
- It was like, "Wait, I
have this kanji test?"

01:00:08.069 --> 01:00:09.889
Like, "I have four
kanji tests coming up?!"

01:00:09.889 --> 01:00:10.890
(guys laughing)

01:00:10.890 --> 01:00:11.970
"What's a break?"

01:00:11.970 --> 01:00:13.973
- So, how long did you do
that for? Until what age?

01:00:13.973 --> 01:00:15.660
- Until middle school.

01:00:15.660 --> 01:00:17.326
- Fuck.
- Until end of middle school.

01:00:17.326 --> 01:00:18.758
So, I graduated middle school, and then--

01:00:18.758 --> 01:00:19.591
- That's like 14? 13?

01:00:19.591 --> 01:00:20.620
- 13, yeah.

01:00:20.620 --> 01:00:23.787
And then when high school came around,

01:00:24.761 --> 01:00:26.790
my parents and I got
together, and they were like,

01:00:26.790 --> 01:00:29.002
"Do you want to go for
a Japanese university

01:00:29.002 --> 01:00:30.381
"or an American university?"

01:00:30.381 --> 01:00:32.311
Like, "Where do you
want to, like, end up?"

01:00:32.311 --> 01:00:33.211
- Right.

01:00:33.211 --> 01:00:35.031
- And at the time, I was
like, "America, duh."

01:00:35.031 --> 01:00:37.071
'Cause like, there's this
boy I have a crush on!

01:00:37.071 --> 01:00:38.000
(guys laughing)

01:00:38.000 --> 01:00:41.265
Like, I'm gonna be here forever.

01:00:41.265 --> 01:00:43.761
So, also, like, I only knew of Japan

01:00:43.761 --> 01:00:45.522
during the summer and winter,

01:00:45.522 --> 01:00:46.980
so I was very conscious that, like,

01:00:46.980 --> 01:00:49.222
I don't know how to live in Japan.

01:00:49.222 --> 01:00:50.555
- Well, yeah, 'cause you experienced

01:00:50.555 --> 01:00:51.942
the two extremes, right?

01:00:51.942 --> 01:00:54.771
- Yeah! And I was like, "This isn't fun!"

01:00:54.771 --> 01:00:55.740
Well, it was, it was.

01:00:55.740 --> 01:00:58.244
But, I was very aware that

01:00:58.244 --> 01:00:59.977
I don't know how to live in Japan.

01:00:59.977 --> 01:01:03.835
And also my English was way
more superior than my Japanese.

01:01:03.835 --> 01:01:05.637
Even after all that schooling.

01:01:05.637 --> 01:01:07.095
Maybe I'm just dumb.

01:01:07.095 --> 01:01:08.701
But yeah, so I decided I wanted

01:01:08.701 --> 01:01:10.037
to go to an American university.

01:01:10.037 --> 01:01:12.838
So, that allowed me to drop
all of my Japanese stuff,

01:01:12.838 --> 01:01:16.097
because, you know, in America,
you study for the SATs

01:01:16.097 --> 01:01:17.238
and you prepare really hard.

01:01:17.238 --> 01:01:19.369
So, yeah, that's what happened.

01:01:19.369 --> 01:01:24.142
And then, 20-ha years later,
I moved back to Japan!

01:01:24.142 --> 01:01:25.568
- Full circle.

01:01:25.568 --> 01:01:26.457
- Yeah!

01:01:26.457 --> 01:01:28.700
I never, ever in my life thought

01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:30.479
I would want to be living in Japan.

01:01:30.479 --> 01:01:32.751
- Was that how you imagined it to be?

01:01:32.751 --> 01:01:34.160
- Hm, well, you know, it sucks

01:01:34.160 --> 01:01:36.163
because the first year I moved,

01:01:36.163 --> 01:01:37.898
so, I moved towards the end of 2018.

01:01:37.898 --> 01:01:40.981
2018 into end of 2019 was phenomenal.

01:01:41.988 --> 01:01:43.726
It's exactly how I envisioned it.

01:01:43.726 --> 01:01:44.559
(guys humming in agreement)

01:01:44.559 --> 01:01:46.036
Work was flowing in the way I needed it to

01:01:46.036 --> 01:01:47.085
and the way it made sense.

01:01:47.085 --> 01:01:49.404
I was going back and forth
between the US and Japan,

01:01:49.404 --> 01:01:51.753
like, doing hosting gigs here and there.

01:01:51.753 --> 01:01:54.886
So, everything like kind of
tied together in harmony.

01:01:54.886 --> 01:01:56.944
And then, you know, I keep bringing it up,

01:01:56.944 --> 01:01:57.986
like, I can't help it.

01:01:57.986 --> 01:02:00.264
COVID hit, and now
everything is so dislodged.

01:02:00.264 --> 01:02:02.046
And again, like I said before,

01:02:02.046 --> 01:02:03.812
I'm just trying to stay relevant

01:02:03.812 --> 01:02:05.544
so that I can have that back.

01:02:05.544 --> 01:02:07.305
I can have that rhythm and harmony back.

01:02:07.305 --> 01:02:11.047
And so, now, it feels more
like a trying to stay relevant,

01:02:11.047 --> 01:02:12.795
catch up game.

01:02:12.795 --> 01:02:13.962
And it's just,

01:02:15.014 --> 01:02:16.194
it's a little bit weird,

01:02:16.194 --> 01:02:17.635
but I'm sure it's weird for everybody.

01:02:17.635 --> 01:02:19.838
Like, it was a mess. It's
been a mess of a time.

01:02:19.838 --> 01:02:23.318
- I'd literally just moved
here and then it happened.

01:02:23.318 --> 01:02:24.268
- I feel like I've only had

01:02:24.268 --> 01:02:26.748
a collective experience of moving here

01:02:26.748 --> 01:02:28.008
for about like four months.

01:02:28.008 --> 01:02:29.176
- I get it, yes.

01:02:29.176 --> 01:02:30.738
- Because that's the amount of time

01:02:30.738 --> 01:02:32.578
I stayed here before COVID hit.

01:02:32.578 --> 01:02:33.411
- [Reina] Totally.

01:02:33.411 --> 01:02:34.657
- I don't really feel
like my journey in Japan

01:02:34.657 --> 01:02:35.719
has like started yet.

01:02:35.719 --> 01:02:37.519
- I feel really bad
for you guys, honestly.

01:02:37.519 --> 01:02:39.298
'Cause it's like, yeah,
again, as you said,

01:02:39.298 --> 01:02:41.039
like, you guys have been here
for almost two years now.

01:02:41.039 --> 01:02:42.787
But it just feels like,

01:02:42.787 --> 01:02:44.596
I feel like you've barely
gotten to experience

01:02:44.596 --> 01:02:45.429
what it's like.

01:02:45.429 --> 01:02:46.262
- It's so different.

01:02:46.262 --> 01:02:47.349
- I think I'm very lucky

01:02:47.349 --> 01:02:48.696
that I ended up in the one country

01:02:48.696 --> 01:02:50.937
where it wasn't effected too heavily.

01:02:50.937 --> 01:02:52.008
(guys humming in agreement)

01:02:52.008 --> 01:02:52.896
- We do have to appreciate that, yeah.

01:02:52.896 --> 01:02:54.215
- Obviously, you know,
it was kind of bad here,

01:02:54.215 --> 01:02:55.656
but, you know, we were able
to keep doing this podcast,

01:02:55.656 --> 01:02:58.445
we were able to go outside.

01:02:58.445 --> 01:03:00.573
Whereas in the UK, it was like,

01:03:00.573 --> 01:03:01.406
"No one is allowed to leave your house."

01:03:01.406 --> 01:03:02.447
- Yeah.
- At all.

01:03:02.447 --> 01:03:03.766
And they were really strict about it.

01:03:03.766 --> 01:03:05.296
So, I was pretty glad.

01:03:05.296 --> 01:03:06.454
I feel like I kind of escaped.

01:03:06.454 --> 01:03:07.669
But then also, on the
other hand, it's like,

01:03:07.669 --> 01:03:09.428
I feel super isolated here.

01:03:09.428 --> 01:03:10.342
(group agreeing)

01:03:10.342 --> 01:03:12.140
you know, it doesn't
help with the time zones,

01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:13.079
it's completely different

01:03:13.079 --> 01:03:15.662
so you barely get to talk to
people in other time zones.

01:03:15.662 --> 01:03:17.272
- I think this is the first ever time

01:03:17.272 --> 01:03:19.049
I've ever, like, felt homesick.

01:03:19.049 --> 01:03:21.473
And I don't normally
feel, like, home-homesick.

01:03:21.473 --> 01:03:22.602
But it was just more like,

01:03:22.602 --> 01:03:26.671
man, not being able to see
my family and my friends,

01:03:26.671 --> 01:03:29.043
that's different from like knowing

01:03:29.043 --> 01:03:30.462
I can see them whenever I'd like.

01:03:30.462 --> 01:03:31.809
- Of course, of course, yeah.

01:03:31.809 --> 01:03:34.662
- I feel that and this
is technically my home.

01:03:34.662 --> 01:03:35.495
- Yeah, yeah.

01:03:35.495 --> 01:03:37.233
- Like, I haven't been
able to see my family here,

01:03:37.233 --> 01:03:39.044
for like over a year now.

01:03:39.044 --> 01:03:39.877
(Reina gasping)

01:03:39.877 --> 01:03:40.710
- Really?

01:03:40.710 --> 01:03:43.302
- Yeah, just because,
like, we agreed as a family

01:03:43.302 --> 01:03:45.462
to like not see each other
until COVID was over.

01:03:45.462 --> 01:03:46.820
'Cause, like, last thing I want to do

01:03:46.820 --> 01:03:48.093
is give my cousin COVID.

01:03:48.093 --> 01:03:48.982
(group agreeing)

01:03:48.982 --> 01:03:49.935
That would be fucked, so.

01:03:49.935 --> 01:03:52.431
- I'm curious, was there
any, like, big reason

01:03:52.431 --> 01:03:53.522
or was there like a turning point

01:03:53.522 --> 01:03:56.148
that made you think like,
"We're moving to Japan."?

01:03:56.148 --> 01:03:58.645
- Well, the timing factor is that

01:03:58.645 --> 01:04:00.465
I was just really sick of LA.

01:04:00.465 --> 01:04:05.465
But also, during the year
that I decided to move here,

01:04:05.747 --> 01:04:08.634
I was going back and forth to Japan a lot.

01:04:08.634 --> 01:04:10.414
And it made me miss,

01:04:10.414 --> 01:04:11.716
every time I came back,

01:04:11.716 --> 01:04:14.426
my wanting to live here
would just explode.

01:04:14.426 --> 01:04:15.546
(guys agreeing)

01:04:15.546 --> 01:04:17.953
Yeah, it would just get,
like, deeper and intense.

01:04:17.953 --> 01:04:22.698
And then, you know, I never
grew up with my family at all.

01:04:22.698 --> 01:04:24.460
It was always, like, my
parents are divorced.

01:04:24.460 --> 01:04:27.371
And my mom raised me alone,
in a foreign country.

01:04:27.371 --> 01:04:29.412
It seems weird saying
America's my foreign country,

01:04:29.412 --> 01:04:30.271
but it is.

01:04:30.271 --> 01:04:32.212
And, like, I was never a citizen.

01:04:32.212 --> 01:04:33.098
I was an immigrant.

01:04:33.098 --> 01:04:34.890
I'm a green card holder in America.

01:04:34.890 --> 01:04:35.829
I'm a Japanese national.

01:04:35.829 --> 01:04:36.662
- Oh, really?
- Oh, wow.

01:04:36.662 --> 01:04:38.828
- Yeah! I'm like a hard
Japanese national, so.

01:04:38.828 --> 01:04:41.807
Until I married my American husband,

01:04:41.807 --> 01:04:44.129
I was never actually an Asian American.

01:04:44.129 --> 01:04:47.711
So, in a way, I was like, "I
don't know if belong here."

01:04:47.711 --> 01:04:50.628
Like, I know I fake it
and maybe that's, kind of,

01:04:50.628 --> 01:04:52.749
just worked its way into
my actual personality.

01:04:52.749 --> 01:04:56.720
But, I'm like, my Japanese soul
wants to be with my family.

01:04:56.720 --> 01:04:59.387
And my cousin had her first baby

01:05:00.959 --> 01:05:03.709
and I was watching her grow up from LA.

01:05:03.709 --> 01:05:05.497
And it, like, kind of broke my heart.

01:05:05.497 --> 01:05:07.479
'Cause I'm like, "The new
generation has started." Right?

01:05:07.479 --> 01:05:09.590
(Connor chuckling) I can't
even be there for that!

01:05:09.590 --> 01:05:10.799
Like, I missed the previous generation.

01:05:10.799 --> 01:05:12.887
You know, like, my second cousins,

01:05:12.887 --> 01:05:15.368
one of them just graduated college.

01:05:15.368 --> 01:05:17.139
And I didn't get to watch her grow up,

01:05:17.139 --> 01:05:18.847
when I used to babysit
her when she was a baby

01:05:18.847 --> 01:05:22.001
and in the summers in between kanji tests.

01:05:22.001 --> 01:05:22.887
(guys laughing)

01:05:22.887 --> 01:05:25.656
And, I don't know, like, as I aged,

01:05:25.656 --> 01:05:29.720
I really felt like I would
be so angry at myself

01:05:29.720 --> 01:05:32.087
if I didn't take this
opportunity, right now,

01:05:32.087 --> 01:05:34.768
to at least experience living in Japan,

01:05:34.768 --> 01:05:37.259
as a Japanese person and
being around my family.

01:05:37.259 --> 01:05:39.308
And then, it actually did, like,

01:05:39.308 --> 01:05:42.817
the timing was insane,
because COVID had happened,

01:05:42.817 --> 01:05:45.270
but then unfortunately,
my grandfather was, like,

01:05:45.270 --> 01:05:46.390
diagnosed with cancer.

01:05:46.390 --> 01:05:48.921
But I was there for him,
and that's all I wanted.

01:05:48.921 --> 01:05:50.898
Like, it would've killed
me, it would've killed me

01:05:50.898 --> 01:05:52.639
if I couldn't be here for them, you know?

01:05:52.639 --> 01:05:53.588
(guys agreeing)

01:05:53.588 --> 01:05:55.379
And, in a way, like, first of all,

01:05:55.379 --> 01:05:56.601
he was old as fuck, like, it's okay.

01:05:56.601 --> 01:05:58.000
Like, he lived a full-ass life.

01:05:58.000 --> 01:05:58.880
(guys laughing)

01:05:58.880 --> 01:06:00.058
And he smoked like a
pack of cigarettes a day.

01:06:00.058 --> 01:06:02.194
Like, it's fine.

01:06:02.194 --> 01:06:06.118
But, like, the fact
that I got to be there,

01:06:06.118 --> 01:06:10.038
and I don't have to feel
like there was this big hole

01:06:10.038 --> 01:06:11.192
in my life again.

01:06:11.192 --> 01:06:13.371
'Cause I lost my
grandparents on my dad's side

01:06:13.371 --> 01:06:15.274
when I was younger and I was in college,

01:06:15.274 --> 01:06:17.573
and I couldn't get out of college.

01:06:17.573 --> 01:06:19.694
I couldn't take time off to go see them.

01:06:19.694 --> 01:06:23.383
So, that was, like, always
such a huge stain in my life.

01:06:23.383 --> 01:06:26.103
And, yeah, I was like,
"This makes so much sense."

01:06:26.103 --> 01:06:27.353
So, yeah.

01:06:27.353 --> 01:06:29.441
- I feel really bad
now, 'cause I remember,

01:06:29.441 --> 01:06:33.295
as a kid, you'd go to some family reunion,

01:06:33.295 --> 01:06:34.637
and your parents, well,

01:06:34.637 --> 01:06:35.978
my parents would introduce me to, like,

01:06:35.978 --> 01:06:38.248
this uncle or something
and they'd be like,

01:06:38.248 --> 01:06:39.650
"Oh, I remember when you were

01:06:39.650 --> 01:06:41.618
"just a little baby! "I held
you, I helped raise you up!"

01:06:41.618 --> 01:06:42.470
And I was just like--

01:06:42.470 --> 01:06:43.861
- [Connor] Who are you?
- Thanos.

01:06:43.861 --> 01:06:45.630
Thanos, "I don't even know who you are."

01:06:45.630 --> 01:06:46.877
(all laughing)

01:06:46.877 --> 01:06:50.650
And now that I've had,
like, cousins who, you know,

01:06:50.650 --> 01:06:52.043
have had kids and I've, you know,

01:06:52.043 --> 01:06:54.414
held them and seen them grow up, as well.

01:06:54.414 --> 01:06:56.913
I know I'm going to say
exactly the same thing!

01:06:56.913 --> 01:06:58.083
- [Reina] You're gonna be that uncle!

01:06:58.083 --> 01:06:59.742
- I'm going to be that uncle now!

01:06:59.742 --> 01:07:02.732
And I know the kid is just
gonna look at me the same way.

01:07:02.732 --> 01:07:04.037
- You're gonna be Uno reverse card.

01:07:04.037 --> 01:07:05.578
(all laughing)

01:07:05.578 --> 01:07:07.515
- You must have so much
street cred with them though.

01:07:07.515 --> 01:07:09.843
'Cause my, like, cousins and nephews,

01:07:09.843 --> 01:07:11.538
they all want to be YouTubers.

01:07:11.538 --> 01:07:12.371
- Really?

01:07:12.371 --> 01:07:13.280
- Yeah, and they're all--

01:07:13.280 --> 01:07:14.389
yeah, I know, right? (laughs)

01:07:14.389 --> 01:07:15.589
- It's adorable!

01:07:15.589 --> 01:07:17.001
- That's why I'm really scared,

01:07:17.001 --> 01:07:19.320
'cause like, my second cousin here,

01:07:19.320 --> 01:07:21.320
he just turned like six or seven.

01:07:21.320 --> 01:07:22.219
(Reina gasping)

01:07:22.219 --> 01:07:23.491
So, like, he's about to, like,

01:07:23.491 --> 01:07:25.131
he's in a fuckin' elementary school now.

01:07:25.131 --> 01:07:26.237
- It's great, I love it.

01:07:26.237 --> 01:07:27.464
- And it's like, all
elementary school kids,

01:07:27.464 --> 01:07:29.264
here in Japan, want to be YouTubers.

01:07:29.264 --> 01:07:31.084
So, I am dreading the day

01:07:31.084 --> 01:07:33.114
where he figures out that I'm a YouTuber.

01:07:33.114 --> 01:07:34.630
- He hasn't figured it out?

01:07:34.630 --> 01:07:35.463
- He hasn't figured it out yet.

01:07:35.463 --> 01:07:36.296
- You're gonna be the cool uncle!

01:07:36.296 --> 01:07:37.129
- Yeah, dude, they love--

01:07:37.129 --> 01:07:37.962
- You're gonna be the coolest uncle!

01:07:37.962 --> 01:07:39.524
- They're like, "I've never
met you before, who are you."

01:07:39.524 --> 01:07:41.269
And then, "Oh, he's a
YouTuber, by the way."

01:07:41.269 --> 01:07:42.383
"Who is he?"

01:07:42.383 --> 01:07:43.582
- Paying attention now!

01:07:43.582 --> 01:07:44.787
- "Which part of the family
is from again, sorry?"

01:07:44.787 --> 01:07:46.148
"Do you play Fortnite,
as well, by the way?"

01:07:46.148 --> 01:07:47.176
(all laughing)

01:07:47.176 --> 01:07:48.387
- "Default skin? Get out of here!"

01:07:48.387 --> 01:07:49.515
(all laughing)

01:07:49.515 --> 01:07:50.473
- It's great, no, it's really funny.

01:07:50.473 --> 01:07:52.188
They just immediately like,

01:07:52.188 --> 01:07:53.524
"Hello! Hi, I'm paying attention."

01:07:53.524 --> 01:07:57.326
- Unfortunately, a lot
of my nieces and nephews

01:07:57.326 --> 01:07:58.296
didn't take after me.

01:07:58.296 --> 01:07:59.456
A lot of them are just, like,

01:07:59.456 --> 01:08:01.627
jocks who love sports and stuff.

01:08:01.627 --> 01:08:02.460
I'm just like, "Aw, okay."

01:08:02.460 --> 01:08:03.685
- Can never get a--

01:08:03.685 --> 01:08:04.747
(Conner drowned out by laughing)

01:08:04.747 --> 01:08:07.862
Never get again, never
get a dog, man, fuck.

01:08:07.862 --> 01:08:08.782
- God.

01:08:08.782 --> 01:08:09.702
- It is crazy though,

01:08:09.702 --> 01:08:10.787
that all the kids want
to be YouTubers now.

01:08:10.787 --> 01:08:13.878
What's it, like, 35% of Japanese
kids want to be YouTubers?

01:08:13.878 --> 01:08:14.749
- More!

01:08:14.749 --> 01:08:15.582
More, more, more.

01:08:15.582 --> 01:08:16.415
It's way more than that.

01:08:16.415 --> 01:08:18.325
- Yeah, 'cause I was googling
it for the UK, as well,

01:08:18.325 --> 01:08:19.158
I got curious.

01:08:19.158 --> 01:08:20.537
There was like, YouTuber wasn't on there,

01:08:20.537 --> 01:08:22.697
it was all, like,
doctor, nurse, something.

01:08:22.697 --> 01:08:23.988
I'm like, "That's bullshit."
- Wow.

01:08:23.988 --> 01:08:24.821
- They didn't fucking give
these kids the prompt.

01:08:24.821 --> 01:08:26.658
Give them the prompt, tell
them they can be a YouTuber.

01:08:26.658 --> 01:08:27.491
I bet they'll do it.

01:08:27.491 --> 01:08:29.230
- I don't know, is like, being a YouTuber

01:08:29.230 --> 01:08:31.090
still looked down upon in the UK?

01:08:31.090 --> 01:08:34.416
'Cause I haven't been
there for like a long time.

01:08:34.416 --> 01:08:36.239
- Is it looked down upon in America?

01:08:36.239 --> 01:08:37.136
Like, would you say it is?

01:08:37.136 --> 01:08:38.416
Or is it kind of like, "Oh, that's neat."?

01:08:38.416 --> 01:08:39.362
- I don't--

01:08:39.362 --> 01:08:40.195
I think it's neat.

01:08:40.195 --> 01:08:43.117
I think they claim it to be cool and neat.

01:08:43.117 --> 01:08:45.225
- I mean, it's considered
cool and neat here,

01:08:45.225 --> 01:08:46.476
for the younger generation.

01:08:46.476 --> 01:08:47.376
- [Reina] Right.

01:08:47.376 --> 01:08:49.135
- Right, but the older
generation are like,

01:08:49.135 --> 01:08:49.985
"That's not a job."

01:08:49.985 --> 01:08:50.917
- That's not a job, yeah.

01:08:50.917 --> 01:08:52.867
- There's still some of
that in the UK, I think.

01:08:52.867 --> 01:08:54.687
- But the problem with that notion,

01:08:54.687 --> 01:08:55.746
especially in Japan, is that,

01:08:55.746 --> 01:08:57.529
the old people that say that,

01:08:57.529 --> 01:08:59.305
are the majority of the
people who live here.

01:08:59.305 --> 01:09:01.635
You know, so it's still considered, like,

01:09:01.635 --> 01:09:05.545
"Okay, yeah, I guess this is
like the new generation job

01:09:05.545 --> 01:09:08.175
"kind of thing, but we'll
never fuckin' understand it."

01:09:08.175 --> 01:09:11.515
- In Japan, I'd say YouTubers receive cred

01:09:11.515 --> 01:09:13.715
when they go on national television.

01:09:13.715 --> 01:09:14.926
Which, it happens a lot in Japan.

01:09:14.926 --> 01:09:16.137
- I've noticed.

01:09:16.137 --> 01:09:17.846
- Yeah, the moment, like,

01:09:17.846 --> 01:09:18.679
(speaking Japanese)

01:09:18.679 --> 01:09:19.512
for example, the prime example,

01:09:19.512 --> 01:09:21.175
started going on the variety shows,

01:09:21.175 --> 01:09:23.686
that's when everybody's
like, "Wow, that's amazing!

01:09:23.686 --> 01:09:25.494
"And he edits all his own videos!

01:09:25.494 --> 01:09:27.247
"He works, like, 82 hours a week!"

01:09:27.247 --> 01:09:29.157
And I'm like, "Bitch, that's all of us!"

01:09:29.157 --> 01:09:31.038
- Well, yeah, I think
from the surface level,

01:09:31.038 --> 01:09:32.745
it's easy to think, "What a
bunch of fucking weirdos."

01:09:32.745 --> 01:09:34.463
But when you actually see all the work,

01:09:34.463 --> 01:09:36.068
and you can actually see they're

01:09:36.068 --> 01:09:39.584
talented at what they do, I
mean, not me, but (laughs)

01:09:39.584 --> 01:09:41.476
you know, you can really
appreciate what's gone into it.

01:09:41.476 --> 01:09:42.614
- It is hilarious to see,
like, all these, like,

01:09:42.614 --> 01:09:44.969
traditionalist, like,
Japanese TV presenters

01:09:44.969 --> 01:09:47.857
being like, "Wait, you're your own editor?

01:09:47.857 --> 01:09:50.017
"And your own producer?
And your own host?"

01:09:50.017 --> 01:09:50.850
- Cameraman?

01:09:50.850 --> 01:09:52.683
- "And your own cameraman?"

01:09:52.683 --> 01:09:54.689
"Nah, nah, we don't believe that shit."

01:09:54.689 --> 01:09:56.530
- Can I just go on a
quick tangent, real quick,

01:09:56.530 --> 01:09:58.204
about Japanese TV? 'Cause I
was watching the other day.

01:09:58.204 --> 01:09:59.037
- Oh, please do.

01:09:59.037 --> 01:10:00.697
- So, you know the whole meme where,

01:10:00.697 --> 01:10:01.768
"hee hee they react to everything."

01:10:01.768 --> 01:10:02.686
- [Joey] Yeah.

01:10:02.686 --> 01:10:03.989
- I didn't realize they
react to everything.

01:10:03.989 --> 01:10:05.097
(guys agreeing)

01:10:05.097 --> 01:10:06.308
(Reina speaking Japanese)

01:10:06.308 --> 01:10:07.389
(guys laughing)

01:10:07.389 --> 01:10:09.129
- So, it was this really, like,

01:10:09.129 --> 01:10:11.141
I think someone had
died, some famous person,

01:10:11.141 --> 01:10:12.986
and I didn't realize,
they had a reaction cam

01:10:12.986 --> 01:10:14.086
for the fucking funeral.

01:10:14.086 --> 01:10:16.255
And I'm, like, I'm sitting there,

01:10:16.255 --> 01:10:17.865
and I'm thinking, like, "This is--"

01:10:17.865 --> 01:10:19.345
How uncomfortable would you be as the host

01:10:19.345 --> 01:10:21.517
where they're like, "React,
react to this guy's death.

01:10:21.517 --> 01:10:24.300
"This guy's crying at the funeral, react!"

01:10:24.300 --> 01:10:25.731
- Yeah, wow.

01:10:25.731 --> 01:10:26.564
- I was like, "Am I missing--?"

01:10:26.564 --> 01:10:28.305
Like, I don't want to be rude,
maybe I'm missing something,

01:10:28.305 --> 01:10:30.814
but I'm pretty sure there's nothing, like,

01:10:30.814 --> 01:10:32.848
in Japanese culture that is
like, "You should be reacting."

01:10:32.848 --> 01:10:33.785
- [Joey] Oh, no, no.

01:10:33.785 --> 01:10:35.573
I've been on Japanese TV multiple times,

01:10:35.573 --> 01:10:36.705
it is corrupt as fuck.

01:10:36.705 --> 01:10:39.315
- And, like, I get why
the reaction cam is there,

01:10:39.315 --> 01:10:41.085
but i was just thinking, I'm like,

01:10:41.085 --> 01:10:42.854
"Maybe you, like, should just turn it off

01:10:42.854 --> 01:10:44.046
"for this one story?"

01:10:44.046 --> 01:10:45.776
- I've seen YouTubers
get canceled for less.

01:10:45.776 --> 01:10:47.795
- There's literally just a corner,

01:10:47.795 --> 01:10:48.854
and the guy is just like.

01:10:48.854 --> 01:10:50.614
(all laughing)

01:10:50.614 --> 01:10:52.046
While, like, it's just
a funeral's playing.

01:10:52.046 --> 01:10:53.817
And I'm, like, doing my laundry,

01:10:53.817 --> 01:10:56.285
I'm like, "What the fuck? What is this?"

01:10:56.285 --> 01:10:57.394
- [Joey] It's really weird.

01:10:57.394 --> 01:10:58.615
- It's a little lot, isn't it?

01:10:58.615 --> 01:10:59.448
Sorry going onto a tangent.

01:10:59.448 --> 01:11:00.365
- No, no, no.

01:11:00.365 --> 01:11:02.078
So much of Japanese TV
is really hypocritical

01:11:02.078 --> 01:11:03.894
to like the Japanese culture itself,

01:11:03.894 --> 01:11:06.638
because, like, we pose ourselves
to be so modest, right?

01:11:06.638 --> 01:11:08.806
And, like, private and all that stuff.

01:11:08.806 --> 01:11:09.775
But then it's like,

01:11:09.775 --> 01:11:11.534
"Oh, this person's crying,
zoom in on their face."

01:11:11.534 --> 01:11:14.859
But then it's like, when you're shooting

01:11:14.859 --> 01:11:17.783
like an interview or something outside,

01:11:17.783 --> 01:11:19.565
everything is mosaicked in the background

01:11:19.565 --> 01:11:21.333
so you can't like know anything

01:11:21.333 --> 01:11:22.305
without their consent or whatever.

01:11:22.305 --> 01:11:24.103
It's like there's such a hypocrisy there.

01:11:24.103 --> 01:11:26.410
- Yeah, the whole, like, privacy law thing

01:11:26.410 --> 01:11:28.314
is just fucking ridiculous.

01:11:28.314 --> 01:11:29.585
Like, I remember, oh my god,

01:11:29.585 --> 01:11:31.562
I fucking remember there
was one prime example

01:11:31.562 --> 01:11:33.884
of, like, it was kind of like
a panel, like this, right?

01:11:33.884 --> 01:11:35.623
Like a host, panel kind of thing.

01:11:35.623 --> 01:11:39.925
And I guess, like, one of
them had, like, a water bottle

01:11:39.925 --> 01:11:42.054
but the entire water bottle was blurred.

01:11:42.054 --> 01:11:44.376
- [Reina] What? Branding?

01:11:44.376 --> 01:11:46.176
- But there was no logo on it.

01:11:46.176 --> 01:11:49.397
It was like the shape of the water bottle

01:11:49.397 --> 01:11:51.197
would insist that it's like

01:11:51.197 --> 01:11:53.655
a specific brand of water
that's not sponsoring the show.

01:11:53.655 --> 01:11:55.718
- Oh, like the Kudo Tea or something?

01:11:55.718 --> 01:11:56.606
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:11:56.606 --> 01:11:57.622
(guys humming in agreement)

01:11:57.622 --> 01:11:58.922
It's like, you know how
like a lot of water bottles,

01:11:58.922 --> 01:12:00.383
it's like, "Oh yeah,
that's a Pocari Sweat."

01:12:00.383 --> 01:12:01.466
Or "That's like a whatever."

01:12:01.466 --> 01:12:02.561
- I guess you can kind of know.

01:12:02.561 --> 01:12:05.434
- So, their option, blur out
the entire fucking bottle.

01:12:05.434 --> 01:12:07.193
- That makes it stand out more!

01:12:07.193 --> 01:12:08.133
- Exactly!

01:12:08.133 --> 01:12:10.043
- I can't figure out, with Japanese TV,

01:12:10.043 --> 01:12:12.394
why they're blurring some
things and why they're not.

01:12:12.394 --> 01:12:14.181
'Cause sometimes, they'll show you shots

01:12:14.181 --> 01:12:15.961
where nothing's blurred,
and then the next shot--

01:12:15.961 --> 01:12:16.794
- It'll be blurred.

01:12:16.794 --> 01:12:17.630
- Everything's blurred.

01:12:17.630 --> 01:12:19.451
And it, like, flips between, you know.

01:12:19.451 --> 01:12:21.304
And I can't figure it out

01:12:21.304 --> 01:12:23.520
'cause it's not like in one
shot there's the branding stuff.

01:12:23.520 --> 01:12:24.744
There's branding, there's people,

01:12:24.744 --> 01:12:26.921
and then just completely
cuts to complete opposite

01:12:26.921 --> 01:12:28.772
and I can't figure out why it does it

01:12:28.772 --> 01:12:31.810
and it drives me fucking insane.

01:12:31.810 --> 01:12:33.222
- Somebody's just slacking.

01:12:33.222 --> 01:12:34.374
(guys humming in agreement)

01:12:34.374 --> 01:12:35.863
- They were like, "Dude,
I am not gonna sit down

01:12:35.863 --> 01:12:37.690
"and mosaic three more
scenes of this shit."

01:12:37.690 --> 01:12:40.492
- Imagine you have to do that
frame by frame, you know.

01:12:40.492 --> 01:12:42.692
- And then adding in all
of the fucking, like,

01:12:42.692 --> 01:12:45.600
subtitles and shit, as well?

01:12:45.600 --> 01:12:48.709
Dude, I feel bad for Japanese TV editors.

01:12:48.709 --> 01:12:52.479
- Actually, I did something
with Pokemon last year,

01:12:52.479 --> 01:12:55.038
before the pandemic really went wild.

01:12:55.038 --> 01:12:58.288
And they had me mosaic, frame by frame,

01:12:59.680 --> 01:13:02.841
every single person at a LaLaport.

01:13:02.841 --> 01:13:03.727
- What?
- Why?

01:13:03.727 --> 01:13:05.813
- Every single person.
- How long did that take?

01:13:05.813 --> 01:13:07.015
- At the Pikachu parade.

01:13:07.015 --> 01:13:07.848
- Oh my god.

01:13:07.848 --> 01:13:10.958
- Every single man,
woman, child, everyone.

01:13:10.958 --> 01:13:13.705
Even the staffers, they had me mosaic.

01:13:13.705 --> 01:13:15.617
- Why not just mosaic the
entire screen at that point?

01:13:15.617 --> 01:13:16.639
- I know, and just, like,

01:13:16.639 --> 01:13:19.039
cut out the Pikachu line
that's like walking down.

01:13:19.039 --> 01:13:20.058
(guys laughing)

01:13:20.058 --> 01:13:21.864
- So, it's like Pikachu
cardboard cutout just doing this.

01:13:21.864 --> 01:13:23.209
- I should've just done that!

01:13:23.209 --> 01:13:25.331
I'm like, "It's the same thing, you guys!"

01:13:25.331 --> 01:13:27.698
At the end of the day. But
yeah, it was so messed up.

01:13:27.698 --> 01:13:30.143
And it took hours, and I'm
making it sound like I did it,

01:13:30.143 --> 01:13:31.155
my husband did it.

01:13:31.155 --> 01:13:32.925
(all laughing)

01:13:32.925 --> 01:13:33.857
I made him do it.

01:13:33.857 --> 01:13:35.076
- You know, I was slaving away,

01:13:35.076 --> 01:13:37.136
watching my husband do the work.

01:13:37.136 --> 01:13:39.165
- I was sweating for him.

01:13:39.165 --> 01:13:41.017
- Sweating for him!

01:13:41.017 --> 01:13:41.905
Three Red Bulls!

01:13:41.905 --> 01:13:43.034
- I think we need more vlogs

01:13:43.034 --> 01:13:44.796
where people are wearing
the, like, cones for dogs,

01:13:44.796 --> 01:13:46.526
where you can just only see in the cone.

01:13:46.526 --> 01:13:48.314
"Hey, guys, I'm at, uh, Disneyland today."

01:13:48.314 --> 01:13:50.397
Just inside the cone.

01:13:50.397 --> 01:13:52.935
- Honestly, and then, like,
I don't know why they do that

01:13:52.935 --> 01:13:54.081
for some scenes and they
don't do for others.

01:13:54.081 --> 01:13:57.394
'Cause like, there was never
a moment on Japanese TV where,

01:13:57.394 --> 01:13:59.384
like, everything is blurred
out that looks good.

01:13:59.384 --> 01:14:01.114
- Yeah.
- It just looks like shit.

01:14:01.114 --> 01:14:01.947
- It looks ridiculous.

01:14:01.947 --> 01:14:03.613
- It's like, "What am I
looking at?" "Where is this?"

01:14:03.613 --> 01:14:05.154
What the fuck??

01:14:05.154 --> 01:14:07.194
- A lot of YouTube videos
look better than Japanese TV.

01:14:07.194 --> 01:14:08.414
- Oh, dude, hundred percent.

01:14:08.414 --> 01:14:09.344
- I mean, a lot of their shows are just,

01:14:09.344 --> 01:14:11.614
"Send a guy with a camera to
this place and do the thing."

01:14:11.614 --> 01:14:12.447
- Yeah, exactly.

01:14:12.447 --> 01:14:14.224
- I mean, they can get away
with more things, right?

01:14:14.224 --> 01:14:16.006
Because if it's a big Japanese company,

01:14:16.006 --> 01:14:18.165
they really, really worry about that shit,

01:14:18.165 --> 01:14:19.905
as we find out quite a lot.

01:14:19.905 --> 01:14:20.743
- Mhm.

01:14:20.743 --> 01:14:24.005
- I mean, there's a lot
of bureaucracy here.

01:14:24.005 --> 01:14:26.191
But yeah, I mean, I don't
watch too much Japanese TV.

01:14:26.191 --> 01:14:27.222
I'd rather watch YouTube.

01:14:27.222 --> 01:14:28.583
- I don't watch Japanese TV.

01:14:28.583 --> 01:14:29.852
- It's great inspiration.

01:14:29.852 --> 01:14:31.580
They have, like, really bizarre ideas.

01:14:31.580 --> 01:14:34.620
Like, there's so many cool
TV shows that they have.

01:14:34.620 --> 01:14:36.414
I don't know which ones
I've spoke about on the pod,

01:14:36.414 --> 01:14:37.247
like, I swear to god,

01:14:37.247 --> 01:14:38.283
I don't know what I've
spoke about on the podcast

01:14:38.283 --> 01:14:39.975
and what I haven't.
- We talked about quite a few

01:14:39.975 --> 01:14:41.309
in the last one that
we recorded, didn't we?

01:14:41.309 --> 01:14:43.032
- Did I mention about
the TV one where they,

01:14:43.032 --> 01:14:44.782
basically just, like, go to random people

01:14:44.782 --> 01:14:47.760
and they're like, "Hey,
we'll pay for your dinner

01:14:47.760 --> 01:14:49.619
"if you take us to your
house and show us."

01:14:49.619 --> 01:14:50.452
(guys agreeing)

01:14:50.452 --> 01:14:51.285
That was really cool.

01:14:51.285 --> 01:14:52.118
- What?!

01:14:52.118 --> 01:14:52.951
- They would like go outside onsens,

01:14:52.951 --> 01:14:55.235
wait for the old guys to,
like, finish at the bathhouse

01:14:55.235 --> 01:14:57.061
at like midnight, and they would be like,

01:14:57.061 --> 01:14:57.940
"Hey, take us to your house."

01:14:57.940 --> 01:14:59.319
- What would they show at the house?

01:14:59.319 --> 01:15:00.880
- Just, like, they're stuff.

01:15:00.880 --> 01:15:01.713
- [Garnt] How they live.

01:15:01.713 --> 01:15:02.546
- How they live, like, what they do,

01:15:02.546 --> 01:15:04.212
like, they're story.
- Is it fun?

01:15:04.212 --> 01:15:05.077
- It's kind of interesting, really.

01:15:05.077 --> 01:15:06.651
'Cause normally, I guess
everyone normally has,

01:15:06.651 --> 01:15:09.298
most people have some
kind of interesting story

01:15:09.298 --> 01:15:10.838
about where they ended up.

01:15:10.838 --> 01:15:12.821
And especially 'cause I
think, when you're older,

01:15:12.821 --> 01:15:15.181
you've lived through a lot of things.

01:15:15.181 --> 01:15:16.775
- Honestly, back when I used
to watch a lot of Japanese TV,

01:15:16.775 --> 01:15:18.616
I got like a lot of inspirations

01:15:18.616 --> 01:15:20.487
for my YouTube channel, all the time.

01:15:20.487 --> 01:15:22.491
Literally, like, the reason
why I do so many, like,

01:15:22.491 --> 01:15:23.699
quiz based videos.

01:15:23.699 --> 01:15:25.112
- (gasping) I was just going say that!

01:15:25.112 --> 01:15:25.945
Those are my favorites!

01:15:25.945 --> 01:15:28.305
- It's purely, like, all of those ideas,

01:15:28.305 --> 01:15:30.574
I'll fully admit, were taken
straight off Japanese TV.

01:15:30.574 --> 01:15:33.029
- Yeah, I was just watching,

01:15:33.029 --> 01:15:35.210
last night, there was like a
three hour (speaking Japanese)

01:15:35.210 --> 01:15:38.363
special, where it's like quiz
god, or quiz king or whatever.

01:15:38.363 --> 01:15:42.300
And it's so fun, because
they have all these, like,

01:15:42.300 --> 01:15:46.600
Tokyo University graduates
or, like, post-doc candidates,

01:15:46.600 --> 01:15:47.433
or whatever.

01:15:47.433 --> 01:15:49.391
And they're so brilliant,
like, they're all, like,

01:15:49.391 --> 01:15:50.669
Mensa geniuses.

01:15:50.669 --> 01:15:52.619
And they know so much about everything,

01:15:52.619 --> 01:15:54.380
and, like, even though they
can't, like, let's say,

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:57.196
speak English, they know
more vocabulary than me!

01:15:57.196 --> 01:15:59.499
Which is like really gross.

01:15:59.499 --> 01:16:01.163
- Can't speak it, but I know everything.

01:16:01.163 --> 01:16:03.340
- Oh, I can say, "Mitochondria."

01:16:03.340 --> 01:16:05.183
Like, I can spell, "Mitochondria."

01:16:05.183 --> 01:16:07.253
- I know it's the powerhouse
of the cell. (all laughing)

01:16:07.253 --> 01:16:09.873
- I'm like, "Really? You
knew that in English?"

01:16:09.873 --> 01:16:13.808
Then again, it is kind of like
Japanese English, but still.

01:16:13.808 --> 01:16:15.649
It's really impressive and it's fun

01:16:15.649 --> 01:16:18.982
and the quiz shows are really well done.

01:16:20.964 --> 01:16:24.003
- To this day, I think that's
the one type of Japanese TV

01:16:24.003 --> 01:16:25.814
I still watch to this
day, are the quiz shows.

01:16:25.814 --> 01:16:26.647
- Yeah, I definitely watch that.

01:16:26.647 --> 01:16:27.971
- They seem like they're
willing to try out ideas

