It's really interesting.

02:07:20.778 --> 02:07:21.870
- No, I genuinely--

02:07:21.870 --> 02:07:22.942
- Makes sense.
- Yeah, of course.

02:07:22.942 --> 02:07:24.759
I care about what people
think about Japan.

02:07:24.759 --> 02:07:26.137
- Well, let me tell--

02:07:26.137 --> 02:07:26.970
I'm joking.

02:07:26.970 --> 02:07:28.017
(all laughing)

02:07:28.017 --> 02:07:28.850
Go on, sorry.

02:07:28.850 --> 02:07:29.938
- I mean, like, London's cool.

02:07:29.938 --> 02:07:31.288
London's like really different, I feel.

02:07:31.288 --> 02:07:33.094
But London's kind of in it's own bubble.

02:07:33.094 --> 02:07:34.397
- Totally different to the rest of the UK.

02:07:34.397 --> 02:07:35.982
- You go out of London, and to me,

02:07:35.982 --> 02:07:38.356
every kind of place in England
kind of feels the same.

02:07:38.356 --> 02:07:39.583
I mean, I would say,

02:07:39.583 --> 02:07:41.724
I do kind of miss the
countrysides of England.

02:07:41.724 --> 02:07:43.474
Countryside's really fucking beautiful.

02:07:43.474 --> 02:07:47.606
But for me, I just don't get
any variety in England, right?

02:07:47.606 --> 02:07:48.855
And that's what I love about Japan,

02:07:48.855 --> 02:07:51.003
I feel I can get so many
different experiences here.

02:07:51.003 --> 02:07:54.816
And, you know, I feel like,
especially in COVID times,

02:07:54.816 --> 02:07:57.846
when we have been stuck in
Japan, even in those times,

02:07:57.846 --> 02:08:00.458
I feel like I've been
able to be blessed with

02:08:00.458 --> 02:08:02.977
a lot of variety in the
experiences I've had.

02:08:02.977 --> 02:08:04.757
Even being stuck in one country.

02:08:04.757 --> 02:08:06.686
And I feel like if I was
living in any other country,

02:08:06.686 --> 02:08:08.765
I would be way more bored than I am.

02:08:08.765 --> 02:08:09.955
- Oh, dude.
- [Reina] Oh, yeah.

02:08:09.955 --> 02:08:12.024
- I can't imagine fucking
being in lock down

02:08:12.024 --> 02:08:13.144
in Australia, right now.

02:08:13.144 --> 02:08:15.693
'Cause it's like, dude
there's nothing to fucking do

02:08:15.693 --> 02:08:16.961
if you don't have a car.

02:08:16.961 --> 02:08:19.091
And you're not even allowed
to like drive around.

02:08:19.091 --> 02:08:20.424
Or like, I can't walk anywhere.

02:08:20.424 --> 02:08:22.571
So, it's like, "Well
fuck what am I gonna do?

02:08:22.571 --> 02:08:26.681
"Look at the fucking same
cockatoo 70 days in a row?"

02:08:26.681 --> 02:08:27.674
- What is a cockatoo?

02:08:27.674 --> 02:08:28.774
- You don't know what a cockatoo is?

02:08:28.774 --> 02:08:30.451
- Is it a bird?
- Yeah, it's a giant bird.

02:08:30.451 --> 02:08:33.203
- That sounds bad-ass.
- Don't they talk back to you?

02:08:33.203 --> 02:08:35.320
- I mean, they're parrots.

02:08:35.320 --> 02:08:37.390
So, yeah, I mean, trained ones can.

02:08:37.390 --> 02:08:39.862
- I watched a TikTok of a cockatoo

02:08:39.862 --> 02:08:42.123
sing a song by itself
when the owner goes away,

02:08:42.123 --> 02:08:43.072
and it was amazing.

02:08:43.072 --> 02:08:45.248
- Yeah, we had a pet cockatiel,

02:08:45.248 --> 02:08:47.631
which is like a kinda smaller cockatoo,

02:08:47.631 --> 02:08:50.450
and we taught it to sing the
Addams Family theme song.

02:08:50.450 --> 02:08:51.402
- That's so cute!

02:08:51.402 --> 02:08:53.881
- And imitate the microphone,
sorry, the microwave.

02:08:53.881 --> 02:08:55.750
Like, the microwave beeping noises.

02:08:55.750 --> 02:08:57.859
So, like, it got so
good to the point where

02:08:57.859 --> 02:09:00.203
it would make beeping
noise of the microwave,

02:09:00.203 --> 02:09:02.750
and I legitimately thought
someone was using the microwave.

02:09:02.750 --> 02:09:03.781
- Oh my god.

02:09:03.781 --> 02:09:05.953
- So, every now and then,
he'd make the microwave

02:09:05.953 --> 02:09:07.162
(vocalizing beeping)

02:09:07.162 --> 02:09:08.550
Like, the button pressing sounds.

02:09:08.550 --> 02:09:10.411
And one of us would go into the kitchen

02:09:10.411 --> 02:09:12.210
being like, "Who's using the microwave?"

02:09:12.210 --> 02:09:14.039
- Yeah, that's crazy!

02:09:14.039 --> 02:09:15.330
- There'll be an episode of

02:09:15.330 --> 02:09:18.915
"Joey's Haunted Microwave" coming soon!

02:09:18.915 --> 02:09:20.736
"I feel an aura! Ah!"

02:09:20.736 --> 02:09:23.248
- He used to imitate the
phone ringing, as well.

02:09:23.248 --> 02:09:24.081
- What?!

02:09:24.081 --> 02:09:25.057
- Oh my god, that'd be a nightmare.

02:09:25.057 --> 02:09:27.349
- 'Cause we used to have
still a landline phone,

02:09:27.349 --> 02:09:28.191
like the house phone.

02:09:28.191 --> 02:09:29.340
- My parents still do.

02:09:29.340 --> 02:09:31.915
- And yeah, it would imitate that sound.

02:09:31.915 --> 02:09:33.740
So, at so many times during the day,

02:09:33.740 --> 02:09:35.110
we'd think the phone is ringing,

02:09:35.110 --> 02:09:36.930
and we'd walk in, it's not ringing.

02:09:36.930 --> 02:09:37.800
- Oh my god, that's cute but

02:09:37.800 --> 02:09:38.747
so annoying.
- So annoying.

02:09:38.747 --> 02:09:40.059
- It got very annoying.

02:09:40.059 --> 02:09:44.450
- What if they could do the
vibrating noise perfectly?

02:09:44.450 --> 02:09:46.336
Someone could do that, I knew
someone who could do the zzz.

02:09:46.336 --> 02:09:47.169
(vocalized buzzing)

02:09:47.169 --> 02:09:48.510
- That's pretty good!

02:09:48.510 --> 02:09:51.668
It was kind of, like,
suspicious how good you were.

02:09:51.668 --> 02:09:52.915
(all laughing)

02:09:52.915 --> 02:09:54.017
- I didn't think it was that hard!

02:09:54.017 --> 02:09:55.818
- Yeah, he did go to
the Tenga factory, so.

02:09:55.818 --> 02:09:57.576
(all laughing)

02:09:57.576 --> 02:09:58.409
- Those ones just go

02:09:58.409 --> 02:10:00.149
(steady buzzing)

02:10:00.149 --> 02:10:00.982
- Wow, exact!

02:10:00.982 --> 02:10:01.815
(all laughing)

02:10:01.815 --> 02:10:02.988
- I know the exact model.

02:10:02.988 --> 02:10:05.208
- I've noticed Joey is

02:10:05.208 --> 02:10:07.419
surprisingly good at doing sound effects.

02:10:07.419 --> 02:10:09.289
We were in karaoke the other day,

02:10:09.289 --> 02:10:11.986
and Joey just does the R2-D2 sound effects

02:10:11.986 --> 02:10:13.796
and the X-wing sound effect.

02:10:13.796 --> 02:10:15.551
- [Reina] What?!

02:10:15.551 --> 02:10:19.863
- You know, in Star Wars the
R2-D2, when he like screams?

02:10:19.863 --> 02:10:21.319
- [Connor] Yeah, I know, yeah.

02:10:21.319 --> 02:10:23.694
- Yeah, I can do that
surprisingly really well.

02:10:23.694 --> 02:10:25.063
I don't know if I want to do it now,

02:10:25.063 --> 02:10:26.024
'cause the fucking microphone--

02:10:26.024 --> 02:10:28.160
- It might blow out,
yeah. But later, please.

02:10:28.160 --> 02:10:29.104
- Yeah, I'll show you later.

02:10:29.104 --> 02:10:31.510
And I can do the X-wing, you
know, like the scream sound.

02:10:31.510 --> 02:10:32.510
- What?!

02:10:32.510 --> 02:10:33.782
Why??

02:10:33.782 --> 02:10:35.071
- I feel like you have to do it now.

02:10:35.071 --> 02:10:38.440
- I don't know, I guess
it's just I have a good ear.

02:10:38.440 --> 02:10:39.273
I don't know.

02:10:39.273 --> 02:10:40.106
- [Reina] Yeah, yeah.

02:10:40.106 --> 02:10:40.939
- Anyway, though.

02:10:40.939 --> 02:10:41.772
(all laughing)

02:10:41.772 --> 02:10:43.584
- Not to put pressure on, but yeah.

02:10:43.584 --> 02:10:44.417
- Yeah, yeah.

02:10:44.417 --> 02:10:48.562
- Actually, I want to go back
to how Japan is super varied.

02:10:48.562 --> 02:10:51.600
Because I actually never
understood that about Japan,

02:10:51.600 --> 02:10:52.872
being such a tiny island country

02:10:52.872 --> 02:10:56.622
and yet having, even
dialects be so different

02:10:57.534 --> 02:10:58.442
throughout all of Japan.

02:10:58.442 --> 02:11:00.223
How is that possible?

02:11:00.223 --> 02:11:01.271
Like, how did that even happen?

02:11:01.271 --> 02:11:02.932
Like, how did we stay so separated

02:11:02.932 --> 02:11:05.092
in all of our, like, clans, so to speak?

02:11:05.092 --> 02:11:07.092
- Look at the UK, right?

02:11:07.926 --> 02:11:08.759
- The UK's similar, where--

02:11:08.759 --> 02:11:09.947
- Oh, I guess so, actually.

02:11:09.947 --> 02:11:12.140
- There's like 20 different
accents just in London.

02:11:12.140 --> 02:11:13.051
- You know what, yeah actually.

02:11:13.051 --> 02:11:13.884
- If you drive ten minutes,

02:11:13.884 --> 02:11:15.671
it's totally different accent sometimes.

02:11:15.671 --> 02:11:16.504
(group agreeing)

02:11:16.504 --> 02:11:18.289
And I feel like, if a country's
been around for a while,

02:11:18.289 --> 02:11:19.479
it happens, right?

02:11:19.479 --> 02:11:21.684
Especially because there was before travel

02:11:21.684 --> 02:11:24.295
was widely available, I guess.

02:11:24.295 --> 02:11:27.517
- I mean, in the case with
Japan, brief history of Japan,

02:11:27.517 --> 02:11:28.550
Japan was--

02:11:28.550 --> 02:11:29.679
- 20 pieces, yeah?

02:11:29.679 --> 02:11:32.133
- Well, yeah, it was in,
like, 50 different pieces.

02:11:32.133 --> 02:11:33.979
And each of them was
technically their own country.

02:11:33.979 --> 02:11:35.062
- [Reina] Right, right,
with their own language.

02:11:35.062 --> 02:11:37.063
- It was all founded in like the 1600s.

02:11:37.063 --> 02:11:39.713
So, for like, over 2, 3,000 years,

02:11:39.713 --> 02:11:42.142
each place was just like
a different country.

02:11:42.142 --> 02:11:44.232
- Yeah, it just blows my mind.

02:11:44.232 --> 02:11:47.282
'Cause like, even, so
I'm in Shizuoka, right?

02:11:47.282 --> 02:11:50.591
And I'm on the side that's
rather close to Tokyo.

02:11:50.591 --> 02:11:51.593
(guys humming in agreement)

02:11:51.593 --> 02:11:55.143
It takes longer to get to
the opposite side of Shizuoka

02:11:55.143 --> 02:11:56.460
than it does to go to Tokyo,

02:11:56.460 --> 02:11:58.553
and I went there for some kind of job,

02:11:58.553 --> 02:12:02.204
and I couldn't understand
the shop keep person.

02:12:02.204 --> 02:12:05.373
I couldn't figure it out,
I was like, "I'm so sorry."

02:12:05.373 --> 02:12:07.783
And I have a Shizuoka accent in Japanese.

02:12:07.783 --> 02:12:10.244
Like, I accidentally let
the dialect slip, and stuff.

02:12:10.244 --> 02:12:14.052
But, even then, I was like,
"I can't figure you out."

02:12:14.052 --> 02:12:17.353
Like, "Uh, do you speak English?" (laughs)

02:12:17.353 --> 02:12:19.242
- Yeah, it's crazy, I had that same thing.

02:12:19.242 --> 02:12:21.602
Like, 'cause my family's from Miyagi,

02:12:21.602 --> 02:12:22.700
which is like up north, right?

02:12:22.700 --> 02:12:27.053
And I have so many of my
grandma and granddad's cousins

02:12:27.053 --> 02:12:30.222
who still live in, like,
fucking tiny villages

02:12:30.222 --> 02:12:32.054
in the middle of the mountains.

02:12:32.054 --> 02:12:33.892
Where it's like, there are some villages

02:12:33.892 --> 02:12:35.873
that have their own
village-specific dialect.

02:12:35.873 --> 02:12:36.953
- [Reina] Wow!

02:12:36.953 --> 02:12:38.503
- And like, village-specific words.

02:12:38.503 --> 02:12:41.790
And I remember, one of
them came out to the city

02:12:41.790 --> 02:12:43.914
to see my grandma for, like,

02:12:43.914 --> 02:12:46.423
first time in like 15 years or something,

02:12:46.423 --> 02:12:48.012
and she just came out,

02:12:48.012 --> 02:12:50.637
and a lot of Japanese dialects,

02:12:50.637 --> 02:12:54.297
they end their sentences
in a very specific way.

02:12:54.297 --> 02:12:55.509
- Yeah, the ending is specific.

02:12:55.509 --> 02:12:56.878
- The ending is very specific.

02:12:56.878 --> 02:12:59.628
All right, so, what's an example?

02:13:00.571 --> 02:13:02.350
Like, in Sendai, normally, right,

02:13:02.350 --> 02:13:04.479
we end all of our sentences in "beh."

02:13:04.479 --> 02:13:05.628
- Ah, "so-da-beh."

02:13:05.628 --> 02:13:07.719
- "So-da-beh" instead of "so-da-neh."

02:13:07.719 --> 02:13:10.370
- We say, "so-da-rah."

02:13:10.370 --> 02:13:11.220
It's "rah" for us.

02:13:11.220 --> 02:13:12.059
- "Rah."

02:13:12.059 --> 02:13:14.608
- Usually it's "so-da-neh"
or "so-des-neh" right?

02:13:14.608 --> 02:13:18.147
Like, that "des-neh"
turns into just whatever.

02:13:18.147 --> 02:13:19.948
- Different shit when
it comes to dialects.

02:13:19.948 --> 02:13:21.959
- Yeah, so ours is "rah."
- I've been winging it, man.

02:13:21.959 --> 02:13:24.000
I just say, "so-des-neh"

02:13:24.000 --> 02:13:25.280
and I'm like, "Am I doing it right?"

02:13:25.280 --> 02:13:26.612
I don't fucking know, I
just hear people saying it.

02:13:26.612 --> 02:13:27.445
- You sound great!

02:13:27.445 --> 02:13:28.278
- Yeah, no, that's fine.

02:13:28.278 --> 02:13:29.280
'Cause that's, like, the standard.

02:13:29.280 --> 02:13:30.269
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:13:30.269 --> 02:13:31.332
There's a standard hyojungo,

02:13:31.332 --> 02:13:34.367
like the standard, regular language.

02:13:34.367 --> 02:13:36.853
- But my grandma's cousin, dead-ass,

02:13:36.853 --> 02:13:39.132
she ended all of her
sentences with "nyah."

02:13:39.132 --> 02:13:40.923
(Reina gasping)

02:13:40.923 --> 02:13:42.150
- I love it!

02:13:42.150 --> 02:13:43.602
- And I was just like,

02:13:43.602 --> 02:13:45.761
"The weeb inside of me
is frothing right now."

02:13:45.761 --> 02:13:46.963
(all laughing)

02:13:46.963 --> 02:13:48.391
- It's a real thing!

02:13:48.391 --> 02:13:49.460
- It's a real thing.

02:13:49.460 --> 02:13:51.752
- It's a real anime character, oh no!

02:13:51.752 --> 02:13:53.853
- It's like, I thought she
was like putting it on.

02:13:53.853 --> 02:13:56.932
But I'm like, "Why would this
like 90-something year old,

02:13:56.932 --> 02:13:59.560
"four foot tall grandma be putting on

02:13:59.560 --> 02:14:02.572
"her ending of her
sentences, ending in 'nyah'?"

02:14:02.572 --> 02:14:03.492
- Joey-nyah!

02:14:03.492 --> 02:14:05.301
- Weebs are like, "We've
found the promised land!"

02:14:05.301 --> 02:14:07.071
(all laughing)

02:14:07.071 --> 02:14:08.210
- [Reina] Go to that village!

02:14:08.210 --> 02:14:09.541
- This super tiny village,

02:14:09.541 --> 02:14:11.431
in the middle of nowhere in Miyagi,

02:14:11.431 --> 02:14:12.402
and you will find a village

02:14:12.402 --> 02:14:14.832
where everyone ends their
sentences with "nyah."

02:14:14.832 --> 02:14:16.631
- So, these people who are posting online,

02:14:16.631 --> 02:14:18.812
were secretly from Japan the whole time.

02:14:18.812 --> 02:14:19.723
- The whole time!

02:14:19.723 --> 02:14:20.710
(all laughing)

02:14:20.710 --> 02:14:23.541
- Oh my god, my eyes are truly open.

02:14:23.541 --> 02:14:24.938
- Oh, you know, older people in,

02:14:24.938 --> 02:14:28.558
I don't know if it's all
of Japan, but in my area,

02:14:28.558 --> 02:14:31.141
"nah-ee" becomes "nyah" for us.

02:14:32.540 --> 02:14:33.539
(guys agreeing)

02:14:33.539 --> 02:14:34.372
So, yeah,

02:14:34.372 --> 02:14:36.784
(speaking Japanese)

02:14:36.784 --> 02:14:38.618
right, but like, not
like that though, right?

02:14:38.618 --> 02:14:40.566
It literally is like type of ending?

02:14:40.566 --> 02:14:42.386
- Yeah, so "so-dah-neh"
would be "so-da-nyah."

02:14:42.386 --> 02:14:44.139
(Reina laughing)

02:14:44.139 --> 02:14:46.625
It's like dead-ass, like, I
wish I was making that up.

02:14:46.625 --> 02:14:48.899
But it's so fucking weird, I'm like,

02:14:48.899 --> 02:14:52.436
"That is fucking adorable,
but you're also like 93."

02:14:52.436 --> 02:14:53.364
- A hundred!

02:14:53.364 --> 02:14:54.675
- You're like 100 years old--

02:14:54.675 --> 02:14:57.115
- And you're saying, "nyah-nyah."

02:14:57.115 --> 02:14:58.645
- Which one says "uwu" at the end?

02:14:58.645 --> 02:15:00.357
(all laughing)

02:15:00.357 --> 02:15:01.658
- That's the other village.

02:15:01.658 --> 02:15:02.491
- It's in Okinawa, right?

02:15:02.491 --> 02:15:03.324
(all laughing)

02:15:03.324 --> 02:15:05.574
- They all walk around
like this. (all laughing)

02:15:05.574 --> 02:15:06.983
- It's "oowoo" right?

02:15:06.983 --> 02:15:07.975
(all laughing)

02:15:07.975 --> 02:15:09.820
- That's how they say, "Hello."

02:15:09.820 --> 02:15:10.653
- Oh, no.

02:15:10.653 --> 02:15:12.149
- Oh my god.

02:15:12.149 --> 02:15:14.564
- I'm curious, do you
ever hear a British accent

02:15:14.564 --> 02:15:16.375
or, like, an English dialect

02:15:16.375 --> 02:15:18.256
that you have trouble understanding?

02:15:18.256 --> 02:15:19.614
- Hm...

02:15:19.614 --> 02:15:21.203
- There will be one, I
guarantee you'll be like,

02:15:21.203 --> 02:15:22.305
"What the fuck are they saying?"

02:15:22.305 --> 02:15:24.604
- I mean, honestly, I'm sure there are.

02:15:24.604 --> 02:15:26.482
I just haven't come across any.

02:15:26.482 --> 02:15:27.814
- Cockney, for me is like--

02:15:27.814 --> 02:15:29.294
- That's not even one of the bad ones.

02:15:29.294 --> 02:15:30.374
That's pretty tame.

02:15:30.374 --> 02:15:32.084
- Really?
- But the more north you go,

02:15:32.084 --> 02:15:33.105
the more like, "What the
fuck did you just say?"

02:15:33.105 --> 02:15:35.535
- [Reina] Oh man, I've
never experienced it.

02:15:35.535 --> 02:15:37.343
- Especially in England,
the more north I go,

02:15:37.343 --> 02:15:39.882
you start squinting with your ears.

02:15:39.882 --> 02:15:41.734
Just being like, "Uh?"

02:15:41.734 --> 02:15:42.894
- Because what they do is,

02:15:42.894 --> 02:15:44.654
they start replacing common words

02:15:44.654 --> 02:15:46.233
with just their own version.

02:15:46.233 --> 02:15:48.343
And sometimes it's that-person-specific.

02:15:48.343 --> 02:15:49.176
- Oh my god.

02:15:49.176 --> 02:15:50.375
- But everyone around them just knows,

02:15:50.375 --> 02:15:52.269
'cause it's like, I don't
know, it's really bizarre.

02:15:52.269 --> 02:15:53.102
- Contextual, maybe?

02:15:53.102 --> 02:15:53.984
- We've hung out with
you long enough to know.

02:15:53.984 --> 02:15:55.855
- Yeah, but everyone just
hears the same thing.

02:15:55.855 --> 02:15:57.076
And it's just really bizarre.

02:15:57.076 --> 02:15:59.474
- Can you mimic one that I
may not possibly understand?

02:15:59.474 --> 02:16:01.514
- Like, there's so many British words

02:16:01.514 --> 02:16:02.643
that when people tell me them, I'm like,

02:16:02.643 --> 02:16:03.852
"I don't know what that
means on it's own."

02:16:03.852 --> 02:16:05.064
But when used in a sentence, I'm like,

02:16:05.064 --> 02:16:06.083
"I know what he's talking about."

02:16:06.083 --> 02:16:07.913
- I feel like in the UK,
the further north you go,

02:16:07.913 --> 02:16:10.524
the more the sentence
just turns into mush.

02:16:10.524 --> 02:16:11.783
- Yeah, something like that.

02:16:11.783 --> 02:16:14.625
- There was a post on Twitter,
it was like, "What is this?"

02:16:14.625 --> 02:16:15.961
And it was, like, British
people were replying

02:16:15.961 --> 02:16:17.083
and it was an alleyway.

02:16:17.083 --> 02:16:19.274
But there was like 10 different ways

02:16:19.274 --> 02:16:20.107
of saying "an alleyway."

02:16:20.107 --> 02:16:20.940
- To say "alleyway?"

02:16:20.940 --> 02:16:22.470
- In, like, British,
well it's not official,

02:16:22.470 --> 02:16:23.425
but like, slang.

02:16:23.425 --> 02:16:26.267
And then I saw it because
it was a quote-retweet,

02:16:26.267 --> 02:16:27.100
it was like,

02:16:27.100 --> 02:16:28.727
"Americans must be losing
their mind right now."

02:16:28.727 --> 02:16:31.833
There was tons, I gotta find
this tweet, it was crazy.

02:16:31.833 --> 02:16:32.800
- What do you call it?

02:16:32.800 --> 02:16:34.103
- I call it an alleyway, but then again,

02:16:34.103 --> 02:16:35.130
I've been Americanized.

02:16:35.130 --> 02:16:35.963
(group agreeing)

02:16:35.963 --> 02:16:37.220
Doing this job, I've had to really, like,

02:16:37.220 --> 02:16:39.649
kick out the very dialect specific words.

02:16:39.649 --> 02:16:41.432
- I say "cell phone."

02:16:41.432 --> 02:16:42.417
I say "elevator" now.

02:16:42.417 --> 02:16:43.368
- Oh, really?

02:16:43.368 --> 02:16:44.357
(guys agreeing)

02:16:44.357 --> 02:16:45.539
- 'Cause I used to say "lift".

02:16:45.539 --> 02:16:46.372
- Yeah.

02:16:46.372 --> 02:16:47.205
- Every time I've met an American,

02:16:47.205 --> 02:16:48.049
- I say "apartment."

02:16:48.049 --> 02:16:49.831
- They were like, "What
the hell's a lift?"

02:16:49.831 --> 02:16:50.712
- Oh, instead of "loft"?

02:16:50.712 --> 02:16:51.545
- "Flat."

02:16:51.545 --> 02:16:52.694
- Oh, I'm sorry, yeah.

02:16:52.694 --> 02:16:54.927
- Loft? Wait, what are you on about?

02:16:54.927 --> 02:16:56.029
Fuck you saying, Reina?

02:16:56.029 --> 02:16:57.187
- My bad!

02:16:57.187 --> 02:16:59.427
(all laughing)

02:16:59.427 --> 02:17:00.260
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

02:17:00.260 --> 02:17:01.828
But they're so contextual, though.

02:17:01.828 --> 02:17:03.155
Like, you can figure it out?

02:17:03.155 --> 02:17:03.988
- Sometimes.

02:17:03.988 --> 02:17:04.821
- Sometimes, but--

02:17:04.821 --> 02:17:06.625
- If you understand what they're
saying in the first part.

02:17:06.625 --> 02:17:07.458
- Of course, of course.

02:17:07.458 --> 02:17:09.078
